WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:12.760
Hello, everyone, and welcome
to lecture 6 of CME 295.

00:00:12.760 --> 00:00:14.800
So today is an exciting
day, because we'll

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:20.720
be talking about AI agents and
in particular how they work

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:25.360
and how they relate to the LLMs
that we've been seeing so far.

00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:28.840
And one of the reasons why
it is an exciting topic

00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:35.080
is because this is becoming
an even more important concept

00:00:35.080 --> 00:00:35.900
for all of us.

00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:41.920
And I'm going to introduce that
by taking coding as an example.

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:44.480
So for those of you
who do some amount

00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:48.600
of coding in your day-to-day
work, so I'm one of them.

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:52.680
Up to a few years
ago, you probably

00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:55.680
were used to writing
code by hand.

00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:59.750
And when those LLMs came out
and in particular ChatGPT

00:00:59.750 --> 00:01:03.530
when it was released
end of 2022,

00:01:03.530 --> 00:01:09.310
we used ChatGPT to maybe just
help us with some coding tasks

00:01:09.310 --> 00:01:14.410
but it was mainly us inputting
some prompts into the tool

00:01:14.410 --> 00:01:17.530
and then getting some code back.

00:01:17.530 --> 00:01:23.690
And then as time
went, these helper

00:01:23.690 --> 00:01:26.650
got a little bit
more sophisticated.

00:01:26.650 --> 00:01:31.850
And in 2024, some of
the IDEs we were using

00:01:31.850 --> 00:01:35.130
were offering this
autocomplete functionality

00:01:35.130 --> 00:01:37.530
that got better and
better, that leveraged

00:01:37.530 --> 00:01:42.330
these next token prediction that
the LLMs are really good at.

00:01:42.330 --> 00:01:47.190
And then slowly but surely,
these tools got better.

00:01:47.190 --> 00:01:49.930
So instead of actually
autocompleting

00:01:49.930 --> 00:01:51.650
the function that
you were writing,

00:01:51.650 --> 00:01:56.330
it was actually offering
you to do less and take more

00:01:56.330 --> 00:02:00.820
from your plates and for you
to formulate another task

00:02:00.820 --> 00:02:06.220
and for the tool to actually
just do that for you.

00:02:06.220 --> 00:02:10.900
Up until today-- and I'm
assuming most, if not,

00:02:10.900 --> 00:02:14.780
all of you are now interacting
with a coding agent on a very

00:02:14.780 --> 00:02:16.860
regular basis--

00:02:16.860 --> 00:02:20.740
now, it is able to take
a full task, almost

00:02:20.740 --> 00:02:23.580
in an end-to-end
fashion from writing

00:02:23.580 --> 00:02:27.240
the code to writing
the test to testing it,

00:02:27.240 --> 00:02:31.780
probably deploying it, and
launching things online.

00:02:31.780 --> 00:02:38.780
And so in between ChatGPT
coming out and the coding agent

00:02:38.780 --> 00:02:43.700
being as good as it is today,
a lot of things have happened.

00:02:43.700 --> 00:02:49.540
And this lecture will
focus on that Delta.

00:02:49.540 --> 00:02:53.220
And in particular,
I want to call out

00:02:53.220 --> 00:02:58.620
a few limitations in the LLMs
that we've been seeing so far.

00:02:58.620 --> 00:03:04.340
So the first one is that the
LLM that we've been using so far

00:03:04.340 --> 00:03:07.580
has been constrained
to only knowing

00:03:07.580 --> 00:03:12.220
about facts that were
contained in its training data.

00:03:12.220 --> 00:03:15.860
So as we saw, I
believe in lecture 3,

00:03:15.860 --> 00:03:18.860
that during the
pre-training phase,

00:03:18.860 --> 00:03:23.320
the LLM was learning about
language, about codes

00:03:23.320 --> 00:03:26.300
but also about knowledge.

00:03:26.300 --> 00:03:29.700
And in particular, back
to the previous example,

00:03:29.700 --> 00:03:32.700
if you were to ask just
an off-the-shelf LLM

00:03:32.700 --> 00:03:36.177
about your private
code base, it just

00:03:36.177 --> 00:03:38.260
wouldn't know what you're
talking about because it

00:03:38.260 --> 00:03:41.060
hasn't seen your code.

00:03:41.060 --> 00:03:43.980
And this is the
reason why, if you

00:03:43.980 --> 00:03:47.580
were to take a look
at some of these LLMs

00:03:47.580 --> 00:03:54.030
that are offered by providers,
you would see a field called

00:03:54.030 --> 00:03:56.350
knowledge cutoff date.

00:03:56.350 --> 00:03:59.750
And so in this
example, So GPT-6 Astra

00:03:59.750 --> 00:04:04.790
is mentioned to have an April
30, 2026 cutoff date, which

00:04:04.790 --> 00:04:10.030
means that if you were to
ask that model to only rely

00:04:10.030 --> 00:04:12.750
on its knowledge to
answer your question,

00:04:12.750 --> 00:04:17.029
it would only be able to give
you its answer based on data

00:04:17.029 --> 00:04:20.310
that it has seen
up to that date.

00:04:20.310 --> 00:04:24.910
Now, as you may know, we
had a midterm election

00:04:24.910 --> 00:04:26.050
earlier this week.

00:04:26.050 --> 00:04:29.750
So if you were to take that LLM
and ask it about the results,

00:04:29.750 --> 00:04:31.830
well, it wouldn't know.

00:04:31.830 --> 00:04:34.830
So this is one core limitation.

00:04:34.830 --> 00:04:39.630
The second core limitation
is that these LLMs,

00:04:39.630 --> 00:04:45.310
they can only answer you but
they cannot perform actions.

00:04:45.310 --> 00:04:50.710
So as an example, if you were to
ask it to order a 17-inch teddy

00:04:50.710 --> 00:04:54.920
bear for you, well, the LLM
wouldn't know what to do

00:04:54.920 --> 00:04:57.960
because it's just a prompt and
it's used to just responding

00:04:57.960 --> 00:05:00.120
to that prompt.

00:05:00.120 --> 00:05:02.760
And what we're
going to see today

00:05:02.760 --> 00:05:06.840
is going to address
these limitations

00:05:06.840 --> 00:05:11.280
and motivate how the
coding agents are

00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:13.200
able to do their work.

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:16.760
So we're going to start
with the first limitation

00:05:16.760 --> 00:05:21.400
that we talked about, which
is the LLM being constrained

00:05:21.400 --> 00:05:26.320
to the knowledge that it has
acquired during training.

00:05:26.320 --> 00:05:31.680
So one thought could be to
input all the things that

00:05:31.680 --> 00:05:37.720
have happened between the end
of the training and the time

00:05:37.720 --> 00:05:41.640
where you're actually
prompting it and using

00:05:41.640 --> 00:05:44.160
that information as
part of the prompt

00:05:44.160 --> 00:05:48.240
in order to get the LLM to
know what you're talking about.

00:05:48.240 --> 00:05:51.690
So if you were to
do that where you

00:05:51.690 --> 00:05:56.230
would hit a first constraint,
which is that LLMs,

00:05:56.230 --> 00:05:59.490
they have a limited
context size.

00:05:59.490 --> 00:06:02.130
So back to GPT-6 Astra.

00:06:02.130 --> 00:06:03.930
So here, the context
window is mentioned

00:06:03.930 --> 00:06:10.110
to be about a million tokens,
which is a lot of text,

00:06:10.110 --> 00:06:14.810
but it's nowhere near enough to
gather all the data, everything

00:06:14.810 --> 00:06:19.050
that has happened since
the end of the training.

00:06:19.050 --> 00:06:20.930
So that's the first limitation.

00:06:20.930 --> 00:06:23.970
But let's assume for a second
that the context window was not

00:06:23.970 --> 00:06:30.410
an issue, you would be hit
by a second limitation, which

00:06:30.410 --> 00:06:37.810
is that LLMs tend to perform
worse if they're given a larger

00:06:37.810 --> 00:06:39.330
input.

00:06:39.330 --> 00:06:41.770
And there is a set
of tests that people

00:06:41.770 --> 00:06:46.370
do in order to quantify
how worse it can get.

00:06:46.370 --> 00:06:51.460
So one of those tests is called
the "needle in a haystack" test.

00:06:51.460 --> 00:06:52.000
And so here.

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:56.380
I'm going to walk you
through what is in the chart.

00:06:56.380 --> 00:07:02.540
So here, the thought
experiment is to take your LLM

00:07:02.540 --> 00:07:08.580
and to input prompts
of varying size,

00:07:08.580 --> 00:07:11.820
and that is in your x-axis.

00:07:11.820 --> 00:07:15.940
So what's on the left
is smaller input size.

00:07:15.940 --> 00:07:20.580
And then what's on the
right is bigger input size.

00:07:20.580 --> 00:07:26.940
And the idea is to ask your
LLM about a particular fact

00:07:26.940 --> 00:07:29.740
that you placed in your input.

00:07:29.740 --> 00:07:36.140
And what you do is you vary the
location where you put the thing

00:07:36.140 --> 00:07:38.860
that you wanted to retrieve.

00:07:38.860 --> 00:07:41.100
So that is the needle.

00:07:41.100 --> 00:07:44.980
And so here, what we see
and this is what is colored

00:07:44.980 --> 00:07:48.150
is that the LLM
tends to be pretty

00:07:48.150 --> 00:07:52.910
good at retrieving the fact
that you place for shorter input

00:07:52.910 --> 00:07:54.510
sizes.

00:07:54.510 --> 00:07:59.270
But as you get to
longer input sizes,

00:07:59.270 --> 00:08:01.990
we see that the LLM
here in this chart

00:08:01.990 --> 00:08:04.390
has trouble actually
retrieving pieces

00:08:04.390 --> 00:08:09.390
of fact that are placed towards
the top of the document.

00:08:09.390 --> 00:08:11.830
So I think the long
story short of this graph

00:08:11.830 --> 00:08:18.670
is even though your model can
afford a large context window,

00:08:18.670 --> 00:08:22.990
it tends to have a degraded
performance for larger input

00:08:22.990 --> 00:08:27.790
sizes, and this is one
limitation that we would have.

00:08:27.790 --> 00:08:31.530
So let's assume context
window is not a problem.

00:08:31.530 --> 00:08:33.750
Let's assume performance
is not a problem.

00:08:33.750 --> 00:08:37.150
Well, one problem
could be that you only

00:08:37.150 --> 00:08:40.110
have a finite amount of money.

00:08:40.110 --> 00:08:43.549
So here, as you
know, the pricing

00:08:43.549 --> 00:08:46.710
is such that you pay per
token and in particular

00:08:46.710 --> 00:08:49.230
per input token.

00:08:49.230 --> 00:08:53.870
And that is also what you
can see on model cards.

00:08:53.870 --> 00:09:00.390
And so I hope that these
limitations were good enough

00:09:00.390 --> 00:09:05.390
for us to motivate the
need to not put everything

00:09:05.390 --> 00:09:08.390
as part of the input,
but for us to try

00:09:08.390 --> 00:09:13.830
to put the thing that is
relevant to the task at hand.

00:09:13.830 --> 00:09:17.750
And so this is the
idea behind RAG,

00:09:17.750 --> 00:09:22.830
which stands for Retrieval
Augmented Generation.

00:09:22.830 --> 00:09:26.710
So the idea is to
augment your prompt

00:09:26.710 --> 00:09:29.750
with information that
is relevant for you

00:09:29.750 --> 00:09:32.390
to answer the query.

00:09:32.390 --> 00:09:36.350
So for example,
if you were to try

00:09:36.350 --> 00:09:40.350
to respond to a query that
is about the midterm results,

00:09:40.350 --> 00:09:42.960
well, what you would want
to do is to have as part

00:09:42.960 --> 00:09:47.480
of your prompt, information
relevant to the election

00:09:47.480 --> 00:09:51.300
results and then ask your
model about the results.

00:09:51.300 --> 00:09:55.280
So you give the
answers in the input,

00:09:55.280 --> 00:10:00.120
and you ask your question so
that the model can answer.

00:10:00.120 --> 00:10:03.200
So here, the question
is as an input,

00:10:03.200 --> 00:10:06.840
and what you would
want is to find a way

00:10:06.840 --> 00:10:10.000
to retrieve relevant
pieces of information,

00:10:10.000 --> 00:10:12.760
which you would
put in the prompt

00:10:12.760 --> 00:10:15.720
so that the model can answer.

00:10:15.720 --> 00:10:18.880
So let's go one other
time to just motivate

00:10:18.880 --> 00:10:23.180
the acronym which is RAG,
retrieve, augment, and generate.

00:10:23.180 --> 00:10:27.360
So first, you retrieve
relevant documents

00:10:27.360 --> 00:10:31.560
by comparing the query with
documents in your knowledge

00:10:31.560 --> 00:10:33.560
base.

00:10:33.560 --> 00:10:35.280
And then the second
one is you would

00:10:35.280 --> 00:10:37.720
retrieve those
relevant documents

00:10:37.720 --> 00:10:42.490
and augment your
prompt and generate

00:10:42.490 --> 00:10:47.870
the response with the augmented
prompts and have your response.

00:10:47.870 --> 00:10:49.530
Yes.

00:10:49.530 --> 00:10:53.810
So the question is, is the
retrieved information something

00:10:53.810 --> 00:10:57.070
that we also consider as
part of the cost calculation?

00:10:57.070 --> 00:10:58.650
The answer is yes.

00:10:58.650 --> 00:11:02.510
So speaking of costs, so
this is one hidden cost.

00:11:02.510 --> 00:11:04.450
The second hidden
cost that we've

00:11:04.450 --> 00:11:06.050
seen I believe a
few lectures ago

00:11:06.050 --> 00:11:10.650
is whenever your model reasons,
so those reasoning tokens

00:11:10.650 --> 00:11:12.030
are not always shown to you.

00:11:12.030 --> 00:11:13.970
These are also
built. So yeah, this

00:11:13.970 --> 00:11:18.530
is also part of the build,
which is the reason why

00:11:18.530 --> 00:11:23.870
you would want that retrieval
to be as optimized as possible.

00:11:23.870 --> 00:11:27.490
And we will see what
we mean by optimize.

00:11:27.490 --> 00:11:28.690
Yep.

00:11:28.690 --> 00:11:29.190
Yep.

00:11:29.190 --> 00:11:31.482
So the question is, how do
we tell the model that these

00:11:31.482 --> 00:11:32.470
are retrieved tokens?

00:11:32.470 --> 00:11:34.190
So there are multiple
ways to do that.

00:11:34.190 --> 00:11:39.780
But you would typically
have some special tags

00:11:39.780 --> 00:11:40.940
in order to say that.

00:11:40.940 --> 00:11:42.560
We're going to
see some examples.

00:11:42.560 --> 00:11:47.060
So hopefully, that's going
to become a bit more clear.

00:11:47.060 --> 00:11:47.820
Cool.

00:11:47.820 --> 00:11:49.680
So this is the idea.

00:11:49.680 --> 00:11:53.740
And as you saw, the piece
that is the most critical

00:11:53.740 --> 00:11:55.960
for this to work
is the first step,

00:11:55.960 --> 00:11:58.380
which is the retrieval
step, and that is the reason

00:11:58.380 --> 00:12:01.420
why we're going to focus on it.

00:12:01.420 --> 00:12:05.260
So one complexity
that I will call out

00:12:05.260 --> 00:12:09.460
is that for natural
language tasks,

00:12:09.460 --> 00:12:14.740
you can say something
in different ways

00:12:14.740 --> 00:12:17.980
without using the specific words
that, let's say, the question

00:12:17.980 --> 00:12:19.180
has used.

00:12:19.180 --> 00:12:21.780
So there is this
dimension of complexity

00:12:21.780 --> 00:12:24.220
that you would want to handle.

00:12:24.220 --> 00:12:28.060
And so here, if you
want to simply retrieve

00:12:28.060 --> 00:12:31.060
documents that have
some exact match based

00:12:31.060 --> 00:12:33.860
on words that are in
the query, well, it

00:12:33.860 --> 00:12:36.390
would not be sufficient.

00:12:36.390 --> 00:12:40.030
So we're going to see
how we can do that.

00:12:40.030 --> 00:12:45.270
So the idea is for us to
have an easy time retrieving

00:12:45.270 --> 00:12:47.070
relevant documents.

00:12:47.070 --> 00:12:51.990
And so the natural first step
is for us to actually create

00:12:51.990 --> 00:12:53.670
that knowledge base.

00:12:53.670 --> 00:12:56.030
And so how we would
want to do that?

00:12:56.030 --> 00:13:00.230
Well, we would first
collect documents

00:13:00.230 --> 00:13:04.110
that we believe are going to
be important for the task.

00:13:04.110 --> 00:13:08.125
And here, what we're going
to do is to process them.

00:13:08.125 --> 00:13:09.250
And how do we process them?

00:13:09.250 --> 00:13:12.750
So we would divide
them into chunks--

00:13:12.750 --> 00:13:16.990
so these little parts
are called chunks--

00:13:16.990 --> 00:13:21.030
and we would represent
each of those chunks

00:13:21.030 --> 00:13:24.590
with some embedding vector.

00:13:24.590 --> 00:13:28.630
And so the process of doing
that is a function of some

00:13:28.630 --> 00:13:30.870
hyperparameters
that you can choose.

00:13:30.870 --> 00:13:35.560
So for instance, the
size of your embedding,

00:13:35.560 --> 00:13:39.840
how big you want each little
piece to be so the chunk size,

00:13:39.840 --> 00:13:43.840
and how much overlap you want
to have between your chunks.

00:13:43.840 --> 00:13:45.640
So I'll give a little
bit of motivation

00:13:45.640 --> 00:13:49.560
behind what can influence
your decision for each

00:13:49.560 --> 00:13:51.840
of these hyperparameters.

00:13:51.840 --> 00:13:55.360
So the first one
regarding embedding size.

00:13:55.360 --> 00:14:01.800
So you can think of it as if
you feel like your documents are

00:14:01.800 --> 00:14:04.800
challenging to
understand or nuanced,

00:14:04.800 --> 00:14:08.200
you'd probably want to have
an embedding size that's

00:14:08.200 --> 00:14:09.340
bigger than not.

00:14:09.340 --> 00:14:15.200
So typically embedding size here
is on the order of thousands.

00:14:15.200 --> 00:14:19.040
The second one, the chunk
size, it is a function of--

00:14:19.040 --> 00:14:21.160
well, you don't want the
chunk size to be too big.

00:14:21.160 --> 00:14:25.000
Because as we said, we don't
want the input to be too big.

00:14:25.000 --> 00:14:28.400
So typically we choose it to
be on the order of hundreds

00:14:28.400 --> 00:14:30.280
of tokens.

00:14:30.280 --> 00:14:34.200
And then the overlap, it's also
a hyperparameter typically 10%

00:14:34.200 --> 00:14:36.060
overlap between
different chunks,

00:14:36.060 --> 00:14:40.360
so that you are not lost
in the context behind what

00:14:40.360 --> 00:14:42.560
is in the document.

00:14:42.560 --> 00:14:44.080
Yeah.

00:14:44.080 --> 00:14:46.900
So the question is why would
we have overlap between chunks?

00:14:46.900 --> 00:14:47.980
So it's a great question.

00:14:47.980 --> 00:14:52.720
So there is information
that sometimes

00:14:52.720 --> 00:14:56.640
could be unintentionally cut
if you were to just divide

00:14:56.640 --> 00:14:59.880
your chunks in a naive fashion.

00:14:59.880 --> 00:15:02.240
And so what you
want is to preserve

00:15:02.240 --> 00:15:04.560
some of these information
to have more chances

00:15:04.560 --> 00:15:06.840
to have them into one chunk.

00:15:06.840 --> 00:15:08.120
So that's one reason.

00:15:08.120 --> 00:15:10.520
The second reason
is let's assume

00:15:10.520 --> 00:15:13.680
you're dividing your chunks in
a very naive way, like same size

00:15:13.680 --> 00:15:15.200
without overlap.

00:15:15.200 --> 00:15:18.000
It's very much possible
that you were actually

00:15:18.000 --> 00:15:21.507
in the middle of a
sentence, and it's not clear

00:15:21.507 --> 00:15:23.840
what the sentence is about
without having the thing that

00:15:23.840 --> 00:15:26.840
is right before, which is the
reason why you want to have

00:15:26.840 --> 00:15:28.660
some overlap between them.

00:15:32.090 --> 00:15:34.370
Cool.

00:15:34.370 --> 00:15:38.250
OK, so at the end of
this step, you luckily

00:15:38.250 --> 00:15:41.330
have obtained a knowledge
base that you can then

00:15:41.330 --> 00:15:44.490
use in order to
retrieve the documents

00:15:44.490 --> 00:15:46.650
relevant for your query.

00:15:46.650 --> 00:15:48.410
And so here, I'm
going to just walk you

00:15:48.410 --> 00:15:51.370
through the high-level
steps in order

00:15:51.370 --> 00:15:54.370
for you to obtain those
relevant documents.

00:15:54.370 --> 00:15:58.410
So it is typically
a two-step process.

00:15:58.410 --> 00:16:02.050
The first step is
about you trying

00:16:02.050 --> 00:16:06.970
to maximize the chances of
getting relevant documents

00:16:06.970 --> 00:16:08.690
as part of your retrieval.

00:16:08.690 --> 00:16:11.410
So it is the candidate
retrieval phase.

00:16:11.410 --> 00:16:15.010
And your goal is
to maximize recall.

00:16:15.010 --> 00:16:18.890
And so here, what you
would use is a method

00:16:18.890 --> 00:16:23.210
that is not too expensive,
because it would compare

00:16:23.210 --> 00:16:26.090
your query with a
bunch of documents

00:16:26.090 --> 00:16:30.460
which can be in the thousands
or even in the millions.

00:16:30.460 --> 00:16:35.820
And then the second step is to
take a look at the candidates

00:16:35.820 --> 00:16:38.820
that you have retrieved
in your first step

00:16:38.820 --> 00:16:44.860
and to do a more thorough pass,
a second pass that is around

00:16:44.860 --> 00:16:47.660
ranking those documents.

00:16:47.660 --> 00:16:50.035
And so here, typically
you would use

00:16:50.035 --> 00:16:51.660
a model that is maybe
a little bit more

00:16:51.660 --> 00:16:54.300
involved in order to
capture the nuances

00:16:54.300 --> 00:16:57.620
and the subtleties of
these different documents.

00:16:57.620 --> 00:17:00.740
And at the end of this,
you can choose, let's say,

00:17:00.740 --> 00:17:08.020
top-k chunks that you can then
input as part of your prompt.

00:17:08.020 --> 00:17:13.700
So we're going to see exactly
what those steps are about.

00:17:13.700 --> 00:17:16.180
So let's start with the
step number one, which

00:17:16.180 --> 00:17:18.900
is candidate retrieval.

00:17:18.900 --> 00:17:23.579
So here, the idea is to scan
your whole knowledge base

00:17:23.579 --> 00:17:28.150
and to retrieve, let's
say, 100 candidates

00:17:28.150 --> 00:17:32.350
that are likely to be
the ones that you want.

00:17:32.350 --> 00:17:37.750
So what you could do
is take your query

00:17:37.750 --> 00:17:45.470
and take each chunk, which, as
we saw, has a representation.

00:17:45.470 --> 00:17:51.230
And what you could do is to have
a similarity-based operation

00:17:51.230 --> 00:17:55.910
that would compute how
similar your query is

00:17:55.910 --> 00:17:58.590
to each of these chunks.

00:17:58.590 --> 00:18:01.470
And then at the end of
it, you can have a score

00:18:01.470 --> 00:18:04.590
and then just take
into consideration

00:18:04.590 --> 00:18:08.630
the chunks that have
the highest scores.

00:18:08.630 --> 00:18:13.270
So here, in order for you to
get-- so to your question--

00:18:13.270 --> 00:18:17.210
these embeddings, you
would use some model.

00:18:17.210 --> 00:18:19.750
And here, we can think
of an encoder-only model

00:18:19.750 --> 00:18:26.320
that we saw in lecture 2 that
would take that piece of text

00:18:26.320 --> 00:18:29.880
and represent it with a vector.

00:18:29.880 --> 00:18:34.160
And we call this setting
a bi-encoder setting,

00:18:34.160 --> 00:18:37.640
because we would
pass the query and we

00:18:37.640 --> 00:18:41.720
would pass the chunk
into the encoder

00:18:41.720 --> 00:18:44.320
in their respective ways.

00:18:44.320 --> 00:18:47.400
And the reason why this
is relatively cheap

00:18:47.400 --> 00:18:51.160
is because you can
pre-compute the embedding

00:18:51.160 --> 00:18:53.820
representation of your chunks.

00:18:56.400 --> 00:18:58.200
So you do your chunking.

00:18:58.200 --> 00:19:00.940
You can compute the
representation of your chunks.

00:19:00.940 --> 00:19:05.000
The only thing that you need
to compute at inference time

00:19:05.000 --> 00:19:09.280
is the embedding of
your query and then

00:19:09.280 --> 00:19:11.860
the similarity operation
across all the chunks,

00:19:11.860 --> 00:19:15.400
and you can do that with some
approximate nearest neighbor

00:19:15.400 --> 00:19:16.960
algorithms.

00:19:16.960 --> 00:19:18.640
So that is relatively cheap.

00:19:18.640 --> 00:19:24.210
And that is called
the bi-encoder setup.

00:19:24.210 --> 00:19:25.970
Yep.

00:19:25.970 --> 00:19:31.530
So the question is, would the
chunks change from one request

00:19:31.530 --> 00:19:32.290
to the other?

00:19:32.290 --> 00:19:34.970
So the chunks here
would be fixed.

00:19:34.970 --> 00:19:37.730
The only thing that would
change is the query.

00:19:37.730 --> 00:19:39.770
And so depending on
what you're asking,

00:19:39.770 --> 00:19:41.290
the chunks that
you would retrieve

00:19:41.290 --> 00:19:43.850
as part of this similarity-based
operation retrieval

00:19:43.850 --> 00:19:45.570
would be different.

00:19:45.570 --> 00:19:48.030
But of course, the knowledge
base is not necessarily fixed.

00:19:48.030 --> 00:19:54.150
You can add documents as you
have new facts or new document.

00:19:54.150 --> 00:19:56.110
Let's suppose you have
internal documentations,

00:19:56.110 --> 00:19:58.130
you have new
documents every day,

00:19:58.130 --> 00:20:02.850
the only thing for you is to
make sure to use the same model

00:20:02.850 --> 00:20:05.970
because this similarity-based
operation needs

00:20:05.970 --> 00:20:07.150
to be apple to apple.

00:20:11.290 --> 00:20:13.210
Cool.

00:20:13.210 --> 00:20:17.170
So just to give you
an example in terms of

00:20:17.170 --> 00:20:21.210
whether or not that would
make sense in every situation.

00:20:21.210 --> 00:20:26.690
So let's assume your query
is, "where is Cuddly?"

00:20:26.690 --> 00:20:28.610
Cuddly, let's say,
is a teddy bear.

00:20:28.610 --> 00:20:30.430
And you have a few documents.

00:20:30.430 --> 00:20:32.970
Let's assume the first one
says, "Huggy likes to work

00:20:32.970 --> 00:20:33.590
downstairs."

00:20:33.590 --> 00:20:36.050
The second one is,
"where is Paris located?"

00:20:36.050 --> 00:20:37.970
The third one is, "he
was in a cuddly mood."

00:20:37.970 --> 00:20:40.410
And the fourth one is, "Cuddly
spends most days surrounded

00:20:40.410 --> 00:20:41.690
by books."

00:20:41.690 --> 00:20:47.370
If you run this type
of retrieval operation,

00:20:47.370 --> 00:20:52.250
it may be the case that here
the top two chunks would

00:20:52.250 --> 00:20:54.770
be the ones that are retrieved.

00:20:54.770 --> 00:20:58.930
And it is not necessarily
something that you want,

00:20:58.930 --> 00:21:01.730
because we're going to
see later but the answer

00:21:01.730 --> 00:21:02.630
is not in this one.

00:21:02.630 --> 00:21:07.450
So why would it retrieve
the top two chunks?

00:21:07.450 --> 00:21:12.330
So in this situation,
what we're doing is

00:21:12.330 --> 00:21:18.050
we're trying to match the
query with documents that have

00:21:18.050 --> 00:21:22.980
a similar semantic similarity.

00:21:22.980 --> 00:21:25.360
So what do we mean by
semantic similarity?

00:21:25.360 --> 00:21:28.140
What we mean is
we're trying to fetch

00:21:28.140 --> 00:21:33.100
documents that are relevant
to the query in their meaning.

00:21:33.100 --> 00:21:38.540
And so here, maybe Cuddly is
represented roughly the same

00:21:38.540 --> 00:21:40.380
as Huggy because
"cuddle," "huggy,"

00:21:40.380 --> 00:21:41.780
these are the same terms.

00:21:41.780 --> 00:21:43.640
Maybe where is Paris located?

00:21:43.640 --> 00:21:44.600
It's also a question.

00:21:44.600 --> 00:21:48.460
Maybe this is something that
is represented the same.

00:21:48.460 --> 00:21:55.260
So the reason why I'm saying
this is that in some situations,

00:21:55.260 --> 00:21:59.940
semantic similarity as
a way to fetch documents

00:21:59.940 --> 00:22:03.620
may be actually not sufficient,
and you would actually

00:22:03.620 --> 00:22:09.940
want to have some other measure
that allows you to quantify

00:22:09.940 --> 00:22:14.940
how close the query and the
document are based on keyword

00:22:14.940 --> 00:22:17.470
matches.

00:22:17.470 --> 00:22:21.630
So this is the case of BM25
that you may have heard of,

00:22:21.630 --> 00:22:27.870
which is a metric that
quantifies how close a query is

00:22:27.870 --> 00:22:32.270
to a document by comparing
the exact words that

00:22:32.270 --> 00:22:36.790
appeared in the query
and in the document.

00:22:36.790 --> 00:22:41.150
So in this situation,
if we were to only fetch

00:22:41.150 --> 00:22:45.150
the top chunks based
on BM25, maybe the ones

00:22:45.150 --> 00:22:47.150
that we would
fetch based on BM25

00:22:47.150 --> 00:22:50.550
would be the ones that,
let's say, involve

00:22:50.550 --> 00:22:52.530
Cuddly, which is in our query.

00:22:52.530 --> 00:22:54.630
So here, he was
in a cuddly mood.

00:22:54.630 --> 00:22:56.590
And the second
one, Cuddly spends

00:22:56.590 --> 00:22:59.350
most days surrounded by books.

00:22:59.350 --> 00:23:05.030
So I can also find you examples
where only relying on BM25

00:23:05.030 --> 00:23:06.670
is not good enough.

00:23:06.670 --> 00:23:10.270
Because as we mentioned,
natural language

00:23:10.270 --> 00:23:17.900
is sophisticated as in you can
say something in different ways.

00:23:17.900 --> 00:23:21.880
So if you were to purely
rely on keyword matching,

00:23:21.880 --> 00:23:26.040
you could very well miss
what you're looking for.

00:23:26.040 --> 00:23:29.680
So in practice,
what people do is

00:23:29.680 --> 00:23:32.860
they don't just use
semantic similarity.

00:23:32.860 --> 00:23:36.240
They don't just use
keyword matching.

00:23:36.240 --> 00:23:40.080
They use a hybrid
combination of both.

00:23:40.080 --> 00:23:43.760
So here, typically what
they do is they come up

00:23:43.760 --> 00:23:48.040
with a way to combine the ranks
given by the semantic similarity

00:23:48.040 --> 00:23:52.400
search and the ranks given
by the keyword search

00:23:52.400 --> 00:23:58.400
in order to have a final hybrid
rank, which in most cases,

00:23:58.400 --> 00:24:03.600
performs better than either
the semantic on its own

00:24:03.600 --> 00:24:05.340
or BM25 on its own.

00:24:08.200 --> 00:24:14.030
And so there is a number of
limitations in this approach,

00:24:14.030 --> 00:24:17.490
and I'm going to just
name a few of them.

00:24:17.490 --> 00:24:21.890
So you may have seen
that the prompts is

00:24:21.890 --> 00:24:23.270
most of the time a question.

00:24:23.270 --> 00:24:26.890
The document is most
of the time sentences.

00:24:26.890 --> 00:24:30.130
And so there may
be a discrepancy

00:24:30.130 --> 00:24:34.650
between the nature of the
embeddings from the prompts

00:24:34.650 --> 00:24:39.530
and the nature of the
embeddings from the document,

00:24:39.530 --> 00:24:41.490
especially if you
use the same encoder

00:24:41.490 --> 00:24:45.330
to embed the query
and the document.

00:24:45.330 --> 00:24:47.610
So there is a number
of methods that

00:24:47.610 --> 00:24:54.730
try to also bridge that gap by--
here, there's one method called

00:24:54.730 --> 00:24:57.290
hypothetical document
embedding, which

00:24:57.290 --> 00:25:02.130
creates a fake document
from the prompt

00:25:02.130 --> 00:25:04.610
and does the similarity
operation based

00:25:04.610 --> 00:25:09.530
on the generated fake
document and the chunks

00:25:09.530 --> 00:25:12.520
just because
they're more similar

00:25:12.520 --> 00:25:17.420
and maybe they give an easier
time to the encoder to see which

00:25:17.420 --> 00:25:20.378
one is more relevant.

00:25:20.378 --> 00:25:21.420
So it's not the only one.

00:25:21.420 --> 00:25:24.200
You also have a bunch
of other methods.

00:25:24.200 --> 00:25:26.940
So one other method I
will say is sometimes

00:25:26.940 --> 00:25:31.820
your query may contain
multiple questions, which

00:25:31.820 --> 00:25:36.600
may be too hard to only
capture in one embedding.

00:25:36.600 --> 00:25:42.820
So you can also think about
methods that actually rewrite

00:25:42.820 --> 00:25:47.060
your prompt maybe into
different questions

00:25:47.060 --> 00:25:49.860
and then you can have each
of these questions actually

00:25:49.860 --> 00:25:54.380
be the ones that are used in
order to retrieve the documents.

00:25:54.380 --> 00:25:57.420
That's also possible.

00:25:57.420 --> 00:25:58.700
Cool.

00:25:58.700 --> 00:26:00.660
So that's one method.

00:26:00.660 --> 00:26:07.300
The second thing is that
we talked about us dividing

00:26:07.300 --> 00:26:09.020
the documents into chunks.

00:26:09.020 --> 00:26:12.020
We talked about having some
overlap in between them,

00:26:12.020 --> 00:26:14.940
so that we are not
cutting abruptly

00:26:14.940 --> 00:26:17.540
a sentence in the middle of it.

00:26:17.540 --> 00:26:23.900
But even by doing that, you may
sometimes run in a situation

00:26:23.900 --> 00:26:28.920
where the chunk on its
own, even with the overlap,

00:26:28.920 --> 00:26:29.920
doesn't make sense.

00:26:29.920 --> 00:26:31.820
It doesn't make sense.

00:26:31.820 --> 00:26:35.540
So here, what people
also do is they

00:26:35.540 --> 00:26:41.940
try to add some element of
context to each of these chunks,

00:26:41.940 --> 00:26:46.780
so that you can read them
in a self-contained way.

00:26:46.780 --> 00:26:50.180
So in other words,
an improvement

00:26:50.180 --> 00:26:53.940
here could be to
contextualize these chunks.

00:26:53.940 --> 00:26:56.100
And so how would we do that?

00:26:56.100 --> 00:26:59.700
Well, you could think
of a way to prompt

00:26:59.700 --> 00:27:05.740
your LLM about the
given chunk and ask

00:27:05.740 --> 00:27:11.510
it to add some context
based on the whole document.

00:27:11.510 --> 00:27:14.310
So if you remember
last week, we saw

00:27:14.310 --> 00:27:17.870
that there is a way
for us to save costs

00:27:17.870 --> 00:27:21.110
if we are smart about
caching our prompts.

00:27:21.110 --> 00:27:23.630
So here, maybe one thing
we can do in our prompt

00:27:23.630 --> 00:27:28.230
is to keep what is fixed
at the beginning of it,

00:27:28.230 --> 00:27:32.030
so that we can leverage
the reduced pricing.

00:27:32.030 --> 00:27:35.230
So in this example, we can put
the whole document at the top

00:27:35.230 --> 00:27:37.690
and then ask it about
the specific chunk.

00:27:37.690 --> 00:27:41.430
So everything up to
right before that chunk

00:27:41.430 --> 00:27:45.550
is fixed and then
everything after that

00:27:45.550 --> 00:27:48.230
is something that
would not qualify

00:27:48.230 --> 00:27:51.990
as part of the caching pricing.

00:27:51.990 --> 00:27:54.670
And so yeah, just to give
you an order of magnitude

00:27:54.670 --> 00:27:57.090
of this caching pricing.

00:27:57.090 --> 00:28:02.510
So here, if we look at the
prices for OpenAI models,

00:28:02.510 --> 00:28:06.180
the input is $10 I believe
per million tokens,

00:28:06.180 --> 00:28:11.040
but then the cached input is 10
times smaller than that, which

00:28:11.040 --> 00:28:16.040
is pretty meaningful saving.

00:28:16.040 --> 00:28:16.540
OK.

00:28:16.540 --> 00:28:21.400
So up until now, we've
seen the first step,

00:28:21.400 --> 00:28:28.320
which is to retrieve candidates
that we think could be relevant.

00:28:28.320 --> 00:28:34.080
So do you have any remaining
questions on that first step?

00:28:34.080 --> 00:28:36.360
All good?

00:28:36.360 --> 00:28:36.880
OK.

00:28:36.880 --> 00:28:39.520
So let's go to the
second step, which is now

00:28:39.520 --> 00:28:42.520
that you have all the chunks
that you have retrieved so

00:28:42.520 --> 00:28:46.760
on the order of, let's
say, 100, now your goal is

00:28:46.760 --> 00:28:48.500
to actually rank them.

00:28:48.500 --> 00:28:51.060
So that second step
is called ranking

00:28:51.060 --> 00:28:53.360
but also sometimes
called reranking,

00:28:53.360 --> 00:28:55.560
because you can think
of the first step

00:28:55.560 --> 00:28:58.440
as also being some
kind of ranking.

00:28:58.440 --> 00:29:02.400
And here, the idea
is to try to capture

00:29:02.400 --> 00:29:07.050
more nuanced pieces
of information

00:29:07.050 --> 00:29:10.770
between the query
and the document.

00:29:10.770 --> 00:29:13.170
And you can afford to
do that, because the set

00:29:13.170 --> 00:29:18.290
of chunks that you're comparing
your query to is much smaller.

00:29:18.290 --> 00:29:20.070
It is not a million.

00:29:20.070 --> 00:29:22.330
It is 100.

00:29:22.330 --> 00:29:27.610
And so here, what you could
do is to take your query

00:29:27.610 --> 00:29:31.770
and take each of the
retrieved chunks.

00:29:31.770 --> 00:29:39.290
But instead of passing them
through an encoder, each of them

00:29:39.290 --> 00:29:46.170
separately, pass them to
a single, shared encoder

00:29:46.170 --> 00:29:48.770
in order to obtain your score.

00:29:48.770 --> 00:29:55.210
So the problem with passing
the query and the document

00:29:55.210 --> 00:30:01.290
into encoders separately is that
you're representing the query

00:30:01.290 --> 00:30:06.220
and you're representing the
document each with a vector.

00:30:06.220 --> 00:30:09.460
And this may remove
some of the nuances that

00:30:09.460 --> 00:30:14.780
are captured by some of the
tension relationships that

00:30:14.780 --> 00:30:17.980
are in between your model.

00:30:17.980 --> 00:30:22.580
So if you actually pass
the query and the document

00:30:22.580 --> 00:30:26.820
through one, single model,
what you allow your model to do

00:30:26.820 --> 00:30:32.220
is to actually model
relationships between tokens,

00:30:32.220 --> 00:30:36.900
let's say, from the query to
the document and vice versa.

00:30:36.900 --> 00:30:41.100
And this allows you to
just be more expressive

00:30:41.100 --> 00:30:46.038
and be a bit more
sophisticated about the score

00:30:46.038 --> 00:30:47.080
that you want to compute.

00:30:47.080 --> 00:30:48.700
So let me give you an example.

00:30:48.700 --> 00:30:53.660
So let's assume your
query is, please find me

00:30:53.660 --> 00:30:55.820
where the teddy bear
with the, let's say,

00:30:55.820 --> 00:31:01.340
red scarf and blue socks is.

00:31:01.340 --> 00:31:06.260
It can very well be that if you
say teddy bear with blue scarf

00:31:06.260 --> 00:31:12.260
and red socks would also be
embedded in a relatively similar

00:31:12.260 --> 00:31:14.260
embedding.

00:31:14.260 --> 00:31:16.740
So in the bi-encoder case,
maybe these two queries

00:31:16.740 --> 00:31:19.380
would have a similar
representation.

00:31:19.380 --> 00:31:24.620
But if you put these
queries along with the chunk

00:31:24.620 --> 00:31:28.180
into a single encoder,
you could allow

00:31:28.180 --> 00:31:34.140
the relevant pieces to interact
with the tokens in the document.

00:31:34.140 --> 00:31:36.260
So by the way, it's just
a motivating example

00:31:36.260 --> 00:31:38.640
as to why this would
make a bit more sense.

00:31:38.640 --> 00:31:42.860
So it is to capture more
detailed relationships

00:31:42.860 --> 00:31:46.220
between tokens and
not reduce the problem

00:31:46.220 --> 00:31:49.580
to a one embedding
versus another embedding

00:31:49.580 --> 00:31:52.420
kind of problem.

00:31:52.420 --> 00:31:53.620
Yeah.

00:31:53.620 --> 00:31:55.860
So the question is, are
the query and the chunk

00:31:55.860 --> 00:31:57.320
passed as one sequence?

00:31:57.320 --> 00:31:59.470
So the answer is
yes, and they would

00:31:59.470 --> 00:32:01.830
have maybe some special
tokens to indicate

00:32:01.830 --> 00:32:04.510
that this is the query
and this is the chunk.

00:32:04.510 --> 00:32:06.190
But yeah, that's the idea.

00:32:06.190 --> 00:32:08.310
And to answer your
question on how

00:32:08.310 --> 00:32:10.110
you would want to model that.

00:32:10.110 --> 00:32:15.470
So one way could be to have
a BERT-style encoder model

00:32:15.470 --> 00:32:19.690
and leverage the projection
of the CLS token--

00:32:19.690 --> 00:32:21.670
so the class token--

00:32:21.670 --> 00:32:24.250
against the relevance dimension.

00:32:24.250 --> 00:32:28.230
So you would want to predict
whether the query and the chunk

00:32:28.230 --> 00:32:30.450
are relevant, yes or no.

00:32:33.610 --> 00:32:34.110
Cool.

00:32:34.110 --> 00:32:35.830
And so the way you
would do that is let's

00:32:35.830 --> 00:32:38.670
suppose you have four
chunks that you retrieved

00:32:38.670 --> 00:32:43.510
as part of step one, let's
say, A, B, C, and D. Well,

00:32:43.510 --> 00:32:48.350
you would put one chunk at
a time along with its query

00:32:48.350 --> 00:32:53.270
into this re-ranker, which
is typically an encoder,

00:32:53.270 --> 00:32:55.090
and you would have a score.

00:32:55.090 --> 00:32:57.060
So let's suppose you
have a score for chunk d.

00:32:57.060 --> 00:32:58.920
You do the same between chunk b.

00:32:58.920 --> 00:33:02.040
And let's say the user
prompt, you have another score

00:33:02.040 --> 00:33:04.320
and so on and so forth.

00:33:04.320 --> 00:33:10.120
And at the end of the day,
you obtain a final ranking.

00:33:10.120 --> 00:33:14.500
So here, you have, let's say,
chunk a that's first and so on.

00:33:18.000 --> 00:33:19.600
Yeah.

00:33:19.600 --> 00:33:22.560
So the question is, what is
the type of model architecture

00:33:22.560 --> 00:33:24.280
of this re-ranker?

00:33:24.280 --> 00:33:27.280
So you would typically
take an architecture

00:33:27.280 --> 00:33:32.400
that is allowing you to compute
relationships between tokens

00:33:32.400 --> 00:33:35.840
and model these
relationships in order

00:33:35.840 --> 00:33:40.080
to have a good chance at
predicting whether they're

00:33:40.080 --> 00:33:41.520
relevant or not.

00:33:41.520 --> 00:33:45.640
And one candidate
for this architecture

00:33:45.640 --> 00:33:49.200
is the encoder-only transformer.

00:33:49.200 --> 00:33:52.520
So here, the idea is,
for your transformer,

00:33:52.520 --> 00:33:56.090
you would only take
the encoder part.

00:33:56.090 --> 00:33:58.690
And the reason why you
take the encoder part

00:33:58.690 --> 00:34:01.010
is because this
is the part where

00:34:01.010 --> 00:34:04.850
you have this bidirectional
attention mechanism.

00:34:04.850 --> 00:34:06.350
And so you put your query.

00:34:06.350 --> 00:34:08.370
You put your chunk as input.

00:34:08.370 --> 00:34:12.090
And you let all these tokens
interact with one another

00:34:12.090 --> 00:34:14.530
through the
self-attention mechanism.

00:34:14.530 --> 00:34:17.570
And you would then
leverage an embedding

00:34:17.570 --> 00:34:21.929
that represents the whole
query and chunk, which

00:34:21.929 --> 00:34:27.409
could be the encoded embedding
of the class token, which

00:34:27.409 --> 00:34:29.370
is very, very similar
to the BERT model

00:34:29.370 --> 00:34:31.050
that we saw in lecture 2.

00:34:31.050 --> 00:34:35.610
And you would use that
class-encoded embedding

00:34:35.610 --> 00:34:40.370
and then project it onto, let's
say, a space between 0 and 1.

00:34:42.752 --> 00:34:44.210
In order to train
such a model, you

00:34:44.210 --> 00:34:47.409
would have some data set that
has query and chunks that

00:34:47.409 --> 00:34:50.130
are relevant, query and
chunks that are not relevant,

00:34:50.130 --> 00:34:51.940
and you would train
this model in order

00:34:51.940 --> 00:35:00.740
for you to be able to predict
the relevant chunks as needed.

00:35:00.740 --> 00:35:02.660
Cool.

00:35:02.660 --> 00:35:04.020
All good?

00:35:04.020 --> 00:35:04.620
OK.

00:35:04.620 --> 00:35:05.920
So we go here.

00:35:05.920 --> 00:35:07.120
We do the re-ranking.

00:35:07.120 --> 00:35:10.420
We have our final ranking.

00:35:10.420 --> 00:35:13.380
So natural question
is to ask ourselves

00:35:13.380 --> 00:35:15.860
if we're performing well.

00:35:15.860 --> 00:35:21.660
And so here, I want to talk a
little bit about evaluation.

00:35:21.660 --> 00:35:29.420
So who among us is familiar
with retrieval methods?

00:35:29.420 --> 00:35:29.980
OK.

00:35:29.980 --> 00:35:31.060
No one?

00:35:31.060 --> 00:35:35.300
So let me go through exactly
how we would want to do that.

00:35:35.300 --> 00:35:39.340
So in retrieval setting,
what we care about

00:35:39.340 --> 00:35:44.420
is that the elements
that we ranked at the top

00:35:44.420 --> 00:35:47.060
are the ones that
we actually want.

00:35:47.060 --> 00:35:52.750
So among the chunks,
for a given user prompt,

00:35:52.750 --> 00:35:55.630
some chunks are relevant.

00:35:55.630 --> 00:35:58.310
Some chunks are not relevant.

00:35:58.310 --> 00:36:02.150
So these are the true
relevant documents.

00:36:02.150 --> 00:36:05.350
When you run your
candidate retrieval

00:36:05.350 --> 00:36:10.350
and your re-ranking steps,
what you get is, let's say,

00:36:10.350 --> 00:36:15.910
k documents that you select
that you think are relevant.

00:36:15.910 --> 00:36:23.030
So what you want to do is to
get some quantified number that

00:36:23.030 --> 00:36:26.790
tells you how good you
went about predicting what

00:36:26.790 --> 00:36:29.870
those top-k relevant
documents are compared

00:36:29.870 --> 00:36:33.750
to the actual
relevant documents.

00:36:33.750 --> 00:36:37.910
So put it in a different way.

00:36:37.910 --> 00:36:41.250
You have a series of metrics
that allow you to do that.

00:36:41.250 --> 00:36:46.050
So there is one very popular
metric that is called NDCG.

00:36:46.050 --> 00:36:48.510
So who has heard of NDCG?

00:36:48.510 --> 00:36:49.230
OK, no one.

00:36:49.230 --> 00:36:50.170
Expected.

00:36:50.170 --> 00:36:54.750
So it stands for Normalized
Discounted Cumulative Gain,

00:36:54.750 --> 00:36:56.650
and it's typically done at k.

00:36:56.650 --> 00:36:57.770
So what does k mean?

00:36:57.770 --> 00:37:01.270
It means it only
looks at the ones

00:37:01.270 --> 00:37:03.610
we predicted among the top k.

00:37:03.610 --> 00:37:07.670
So it only looks at
the top-k predictions.

00:37:07.670 --> 00:37:14.750
And what it does is it compares
whether the ones that you

00:37:14.750 --> 00:37:17.310
selected for, let's
say, a given position

00:37:17.310 --> 00:37:21.390
is actually relevant or not.

00:37:21.390 --> 00:37:23.650
And so yeah, don't be
scared by this formula.

00:37:23.650 --> 00:37:29.710
So it just means that we try
to incentivize the system

00:37:29.710 --> 00:37:33.310
from having a higher metric,
which is the ones that are more

00:37:33.310 --> 00:37:35.910
relevant to be higher ranked.

00:37:35.910 --> 00:37:39.750
So here, the discounted
cumulative gain

00:37:39.750 --> 00:37:42.230
is higher if the
relevant documents

00:37:42.230 --> 00:37:46.520
are high in the ranking
which is a lower i.

00:37:46.520 --> 00:37:52.200
So higher ranking here means,
let's say, rank closer to 1.

00:37:52.200 --> 00:37:56.720
And so the reason why we
normalize that metric is

00:37:56.720 --> 00:38:00.560
because the discounted
cumulative gain at k--

00:38:00.560 --> 00:38:02.080
like this--

00:38:02.080 --> 00:38:06.720
is not directly interpretable,
because it is not always

00:38:06.720 --> 00:38:09.100
between, let's say, 0 and 1.

00:38:09.100 --> 00:38:11.400
It is somewhere in there.

00:38:11.400 --> 00:38:16.280
So what people do is they
compute the discounted

00:38:16.280 --> 00:38:20.560
cumulative gain for
a perfect ranking,

00:38:20.560 --> 00:38:26.480
and then they divide the DCG@k
with that perfect ranking

00:38:26.480 --> 00:38:29.940
in order to get a metric
that is bounded by 1.

00:38:33.760 --> 00:38:35.232
Does that make sense?

00:38:35.232 --> 00:38:36.940
And this metric, by
the way, is positive.

00:38:36.940 --> 00:38:40.640
You see a log, but everything
that is in between the log

00:38:40.640 --> 00:38:44.340
is greater or equal
than 2, I believe.

00:38:44.340 --> 00:38:48.450
So yeah, i can start from 1.

00:38:48.450 --> 00:38:53.130
So anyways, nothing is going
into the negative territory

00:38:53.130 --> 00:38:54.330
of log.

00:38:54.330 --> 00:38:57.510
So this metric is
between 0 and 1.

00:38:57.510 --> 00:39:02.530
And the closer you get to
1, the better your ranking.

00:39:02.530 --> 00:39:06.730
So NDCG is one of the metrics.

00:39:06.730 --> 00:39:09.010
The second kind of
metric that you can see

00:39:09.010 --> 00:39:17.410
is Reciprocal Rank, which
takes the ratio between 1

00:39:17.410 --> 00:39:20.610
and the rank of the
first relevant chunk.

00:39:20.610 --> 00:39:25.610
So here, again, the higher
the metric the better.

00:39:25.610 --> 00:39:28.850
And then I'm sure you're
familiar with the precision

00:39:28.850 --> 00:39:32.610
and recall metrics in the
classic binary classification

00:39:32.610 --> 00:39:33.810
problems.

00:39:33.810 --> 00:39:38.010
So here, you have the exact
same setup where for recall

00:39:38.010 --> 00:39:42.450
at k, what you care about
is among all the documents

00:39:42.450 --> 00:39:47.060
that were relevant, how many
of them were you actually

00:39:47.060 --> 00:39:53.900
able to fetch among the
top-k predicted documents?

00:39:53.900 --> 00:39:55.200
And then precision.

00:39:55.200 --> 00:39:58.038
So here, you don't care about
all the relevant documents.

00:39:58.038 --> 00:40:00.580
You care about only the ones
that you have predicted as being

00:40:00.580 --> 00:40:03.340
relevant, which are the top k.

00:40:03.340 --> 00:40:05.740
You look at this
top k and you see

00:40:05.740 --> 00:40:11.380
what is the proportion of those
that are actually relevant.

00:40:11.380 --> 00:40:13.340
And so here, recall.

00:40:13.340 --> 00:40:14.700
You want to maximize recall.

00:40:14.700 --> 00:40:18.540
And precision, of course you
want to maximize precision.

00:40:18.540 --> 00:40:23.660
So long story short, in order to
evaluate how well your setup is

00:40:23.660 --> 00:40:29.220
doing, what you typically
do is have a data set

00:40:29.220 --> 00:40:32.420
that contains labels
around which document

00:40:32.420 --> 00:40:36.580
is relevant for a specific
query versus which one is not.

00:40:36.580 --> 00:40:38.380
And you run your system.

00:40:38.380 --> 00:40:43.710
You see what are the top-k
predicted relevant documents,

00:40:43.710 --> 00:40:47.910
and then you compare those
predicted relevant documents

00:40:47.910 --> 00:40:52.890
with the actual relevant
documents using those metrics.

00:40:59.470 --> 00:41:00.590
Cool.

00:41:00.590 --> 00:41:01.270
OK.

00:41:01.270 --> 00:41:05.030
I want to add one
thing, which is

00:41:05.030 --> 00:41:07.470
that we've been
talking about how

00:41:07.470 --> 00:41:09.250
to retrieve relevant documents.

00:41:09.250 --> 00:41:13.590
But if you remember, our
first motivating application

00:41:13.590 --> 00:41:16.070
was coding agents.

00:41:16.070 --> 00:41:19.990
And when it comes
to coding questions,

00:41:19.990 --> 00:41:22.430
well, most of the
time you're interested

00:41:22.430 --> 00:41:26.550
in particular functions
and retrieving

00:41:26.550 --> 00:41:32.950
semantically similar codes
may not make as much sense.

00:41:32.950 --> 00:41:38.070
In fact, you're most of the
time interested in retrieving

00:41:38.070 --> 00:41:42.600
exact matches, which
is the reason why

00:41:42.600 --> 00:41:45.880
when it comes to coding
applications, what

00:41:45.880 --> 00:41:51.560
people typically do is to
actually retrieve exact matches.

00:41:51.560 --> 00:41:57.120
And we do that with
a specific operation,

00:41:57.120 --> 00:42:01.140
which is called GREP for Global
Regular Expression Print.

00:42:01.140 --> 00:42:04.160
So I'm sure a lot
of you have already

00:42:04.160 --> 00:42:05.580
seen that in your coding agent.

00:42:05.580 --> 00:42:10.000
So it does some
grep operation based

00:42:10.000 --> 00:42:13.840
on some term that is trying to
search through the code base,

00:42:13.840 --> 00:42:17.600
and it finds all the locations
where that term occurs

00:42:17.600 --> 00:42:19.978
and then leverages that.

00:42:19.978 --> 00:42:21.520
So all of that to
say that what we've

00:42:21.520 --> 00:42:25.880
seen with respect to semantic
similarity and keyword search

00:42:25.880 --> 00:42:30.800
is mostly applicable for natural
language-related problems.

00:42:30.800 --> 00:42:34.240
But when it comes to coding,
it may make more sense

00:42:34.240 --> 00:42:42.440
to have exact matches, and this
is how people typically do it.

00:42:42.440 --> 00:42:46.400
Any questions on everything
we have seen so far?

00:42:46.400 --> 00:42:48.000
Yep.

00:42:48.000 --> 00:42:50.840
The question is, well,
if you retrieve your k,

00:42:50.840 --> 00:42:52.660
which one do you
put into the prompt?

00:42:52.660 --> 00:42:56.500
So it really varies
from setup to setup.

00:42:56.500 --> 00:43:01.700
You can very well put all
the k's in your prompt.

00:43:01.700 --> 00:43:03.920
So people typically do that.

00:43:03.920 --> 00:43:06.680
But you could also have
some other scoring mechanism

00:43:06.680 --> 00:43:08.320
and have some thresholds.

00:43:08.320 --> 00:43:10.680
Let's suppose below
which you are actually

00:43:10.680 --> 00:43:12.240
not going to put it.

00:43:12.240 --> 00:43:16.620
But people typically put the k
chunks as part of the prompt.

00:43:20.800 --> 00:43:23.020
So the question is, when
you augment the prompt,

00:43:23.020 --> 00:43:28.600
do you tell that a specific
chunk was closer to the user

00:43:28.600 --> 00:43:31.240
prompt versus another one?

00:43:31.240 --> 00:43:34.500
Well, the answer is I think it
depends from setup to setup.

00:43:34.500 --> 00:43:38.010
Typically, you would put
it in the order from most

00:43:38.010 --> 00:43:40.290
to least relevant.

00:43:40.290 --> 00:43:43.170
But I would say it's very
much setup dependent, as in

00:43:43.170 --> 00:43:44.710
depends on the implementation.

00:43:44.710 --> 00:43:46.790
And I've seen multiple
ways to do that.

00:43:50.130 --> 00:43:51.810
Yeah.

00:43:51.810 --> 00:43:55.590
So the question is, where do
vector databases come into play?

00:43:55.590 --> 00:43:58.450
So here, it would come into play
for the first step, candidate

00:43:58.450 --> 00:44:00.930
retrieval, where
you need to store

00:44:00.930 --> 00:44:04.490
the chunks and their
embeddings into that database.

00:44:04.490 --> 00:44:07.410
But then the second step,
which is the re-ranking,

00:44:07.410 --> 00:44:12.770
cannot leverage that because
always you need to compute

00:44:12.770 --> 00:44:17.330
the joint embedding and
have the resulting score.

00:44:17.330 --> 00:44:20.570
You cannot pre-compute
that, if that makes sense.

00:44:20.570 --> 00:44:23.150
So you would use that
for the first step.

00:44:25.970 --> 00:44:26.490
Cool.

00:44:26.490 --> 00:44:28.170
I think we're all good.

00:44:28.170 --> 00:44:34.220
And with that, I'll
give it to Shervine.

00:44:34.220 --> 00:44:35.780
Thank you, Afshine.

00:44:35.780 --> 00:44:39.780
So now that we have
seen how RAG worked,

00:44:39.780 --> 00:44:41.340
I'm sure you were
looking forward

00:44:41.340 --> 00:44:43.220
to the next stage,
which is to talk

00:44:43.220 --> 00:44:45.260
about how to perform actions.

00:44:45.260 --> 00:44:46.460
So here it is.

00:44:46.460 --> 00:44:50.580
And to motivate that, let's come
back to our favorite examples.

00:44:50.580 --> 00:44:54.700
So first, where is
Cuddly, which we used.

00:44:54.700 --> 00:44:59.220
We said that if you had
to input that into an LLM,

00:44:59.220 --> 00:45:02.300
then it's well possible
that the LLM doesn't know,

00:45:02.300 --> 00:45:07.740
because you might not
have traces of Cuddly

00:45:07.740 --> 00:45:09.920
in the LLM's knowledge.

00:45:09.920 --> 00:45:14.260
For a while, we saw that the
knowledge cutoff of typical LLMs

00:45:14.260 --> 00:45:18.900
are a few months old, so
they are not relevant.

00:45:18.900 --> 00:45:22.880
And here, if you have a
database that is updated,

00:45:22.880 --> 00:45:24.660
let's say, every day
or so, and let's say

00:45:24.660 --> 00:45:27.540
your teddy bear Cuddly
doesn't move that much,

00:45:27.540 --> 00:45:30.100
then maybe you have
some relevant chunks

00:45:30.100 --> 00:45:32.750
that can be selected and
then put in your prompt.

00:45:32.750 --> 00:45:35.870
So here, let's
say, Cuddly spends

00:45:35.870 --> 00:45:38.870
most days surrounded by books.

00:45:38.870 --> 00:45:42.630
So you know where to look for.

00:45:42.630 --> 00:45:47.070
Now, let's try a more
difficult example.

00:45:47.070 --> 00:45:50.310
Let's say you are
somewhere, and you want

00:45:50.310 --> 00:45:52.210
to find a teddy bear near you.

00:45:52.210 --> 00:45:55.130
So you cannot deal with a
static database anymore.

00:45:55.130 --> 00:45:57.510
You want real-time.

00:45:57.510 --> 00:46:01.850
So of course, the vanilla LLM
doesn't know much about it.

00:46:01.850 --> 00:46:03.750
The same as the first case.

00:46:03.750 --> 00:46:10.110
And here, if you use RAG, you
will have some relevant chunks

00:46:10.110 --> 00:46:14.550
that might be matched,
but they will not

00:46:14.550 --> 00:46:18.530
be of help because it might
be data that is outdated.

00:46:18.530 --> 00:46:25.030
Might be data that is
static, which is why we

00:46:25.030 --> 00:46:26.950
need something more powerful.

00:46:26.950 --> 00:46:29.800
And then here, this
example has two components

00:46:29.800 --> 00:46:31.760
that I want to emphasize on.

00:46:31.760 --> 00:46:36.260
So first, this notion of
real-time interaction.

00:46:36.260 --> 00:46:40.200
So maybe you need a real-time
backend to query here.

00:46:40.200 --> 00:46:43.560
And then the second
one is, let's say,

00:46:43.560 --> 00:46:46.320
you have a relationship
to you as a user

00:46:46.320 --> 00:46:47.740
that needs to be established.

00:46:47.740 --> 00:46:51.480
So maybe you need to perform
an action on your own device

00:46:51.480 --> 00:46:54.880
in order to be more precise
about what you're looking for.

00:46:54.880 --> 00:46:58.560
So here, when you say
find a teddy bear near me,

00:46:58.560 --> 00:47:03.400
then you need somehow the LLM
to be aware of where you are.

00:47:03.400 --> 00:47:10.840
So you need some GPS check
that goes on somewhere.

00:47:10.840 --> 00:47:14.240
So this is something that we're
going to tackle with tools.

00:47:14.240 --> 00:47:17.760
So who has heard of tools?

00:47:17.760 --> 00:47:20.200
OK, so about half.

00:47:20.200 --> 00:47:21.000
Awesome.

00:47:21.000 --> 00:47:24.920
So the way tools work is that
they can perform actions.

00:47:24.920 --> 00:47:26.720
And the actions that
you want to perform

00:47:26.720 --> 00:47:30.170
can be predicted as part
of the LLM lifecycle

00:47:30.170 --> 00:47:31.270
when you discuss with it.

00:47:31.270 --> 00:47:35.650
So let's say you want to
find a teddy bear near you,

00:47:35.650 --> 00:47:38.350
then you add the
tools of interest

00:47:38.350 --> 00:47:43.510
and what the LLM will do is not
to give you a response directly.

00:47:43.510 --> 00:47:45.810
But instead, it
will predict what

00:47:45.810 --> 00:47:48.490
to do with the tools
you have at hand.

00:47:48.490 --> 00:47:51.990
So here, we said that we
want something near you.

00:47:51.990 --> 00:47:55.410
So you need some function
that will establish your GPS

00:47:55.410 --> 00:47:56.150
coordinates.

00:47:56.150 --> 00:47:59.530
So let's suppose my position
is the one doing that.

00:47:59.530 --> 00:48:02.850
And then once you have
these coordinates,

00:48:02.850 --> 00:48:07.010
you need a function that queries
a backend and that queries what

00:48:07.010 --> 00:48:11.650
is around that set
of coordinates.

00:48:11.650 --> 00:48:15.530
And the way it works is that
once you perform that query,

00:48:15.530 --> 00:48:18.870
then the backend will
return some answer.

00:48:18.870 --> 00:48:22.330
So this is something that
is not linked to LLMs.

00:48:22.330 --> 00:48:27.550
And then you feed that backend
result back into the LLM

00:48:27.550 --> 00:48:30.850
in order to obtain
a final response.

00:48:30.850 --> 00:48:34.730
So this is really a
three-stage process.

00:48:34.730 --> 00:48:37.050
So first one, LLM based.

00:48:37.050 --> 00:48:40.330
You need to find what
action to perform.

00:48:40.330 --> 00:48:44.090
The second one is the action
of actually performing

00:48:44.090 --> 00:48:45.450
what was predicted.

00:48:45.450 --> 00:48:47.570
And then the last
step is the one

00:48:47.570 --> 00:48:51.530
that tells you back
so tells to the user

00:48:51.530 --> 00:48:57.010
what the action has been done
or what they were looking for.

00:48:57.010 --> 00:48:58.870
Yeah.

00:48:58.870 --> 00:48:59.370
Yeah.

00:48:59.370 --> 00:49:01.010
So the question
is, once you have

00:49:01.010 --> 00:49:04.790
predicted the arguments like
the tool call, what happens?

00:49:04.790 --> 00:49:05.710
Do you have to wait?

00:49:05.710 --> 00:49:07.050
The answer is yes.

00:49:07.050 --> 00:49:10.030
So you predict what
you need to perform,

00:49:10.030 --> 00:49:14.130
then you need to wait for
that action to take place.

00:49:14.130 --> 00:49:16.550
But if I had to tell
some orders of magnitude

00:49:16.550 --> 00:49:20.950
you see in practice, the latency
can vary a lot of the time.

00:49:20.950 --> 00:49:25.720
So in this case, computing
your location is near instant,

00:49:25.720 --> 00:49:30.300
so you have some GPS
signal almost instant.

00:49:30.300 --> 00:49:35.340
Things that have some internet
search or backend search to it

00:49:35.340 --> 00:49:38.480
might be something
that you feel as a user

00:49:38.480 --> 00:49:41.420
but typically less
than the latency

00:49:41.420 --> 00:49:43.620
that you have to
generate an answer.

00:49:43.620 --> 00:49:45.220
So I would say a
lot of the time,

00:49:45.220 --> 00:49:49.480
this is not the
dominant latency.

00:49:49.480 --> 00:49:49.980
Yeah.

00:49:49.980 --> 00:49:52.820
So the question is let's
suppose I'm talking to an agent

00:49:52.820 --> 00:49:54.740
and asking it to
execute some code.

00:49:54.740 --> 00:49:57.260
So I think in that case,
you would not consider

00:49:57.260 --> 00:49:59.260
that as a tool call per se.

00:49:59.260 --> 00:50:01.660
It would be wrapped
as some command

00:50:01.660 --> 00:50:04.780
and then your agent would
be back available to you.

00:50:04.780 --> 00:50:08.480
So we'll talk about the coding
agent part in the last part.

00:50:08.480 --> 00:50:10.400
But yeah, great point.

00:50:12.720 --> 00:50:13.220
OK.

00:50:13.220 --> 00:50:13.720
Awesome.

00:50:13.720 --> 00:50:14.540
Any other question?

00:50:14.540 --> 00:50:16.240
I want just to put a disclaimer.

00:50:16.240 --> 00:50:17.800
Tools are super powerful.

00:50:17.800 --> 00:50:21.550
So if you have fully
internalized these three steps,

00:50:21.550 --> 00:50:23.690
nothing will scare you anymore.

00:50:23.690 --> 00:50:25.110
You'll be invincible.

00:50:25.110 --> 00:50:28.750
So in case you have any
questions here, let me know.

00:50:28.750 --> 00:50:32.890
You have plenty of opportunities
later on as well to ask.

00:50:32.890 --> 00:50:34.310
OK, great.

00:50:34.310 --> 00:50:36.350
Yeah.

00:50:36.350 --> 00:50:38.350
So the question is,
how does the model

00:50:38.350 --> 00:50:40.130
know how to use these tools?

00:50:40.130 --> 00:50:41.570
So you're ahead of me.

00:50:41.570 --> 00:50:43.170
It's just after the
following slide.

00:50:43.170 --> 00:50:44.090
We'll talk about it.

00:50:44.090 --> 00:50:44.590
Yeah.

00:50:47.350 --> 00:50:48.510
OK, great.

00:50:48.510 --> 00:50:53.010
So just diving in more into
what tool implementations are.

00:50:53.010 --> 00:50:55.210
So a lot of the
common providers,

00:50:55.210 --> 00:50:59.830
they use Python as a way
to express these tools.

00:50:59.830 --> 00:51:01.940
So in practice, I
mentioned that you had

00:51:01.940 --> 00:51:03.190
a tool called find_teddy_bear.

00:51:03.190 --> 00:51:06.430
T So somewhere in
the implementation

00:51:06.430 --> 00:51:09.790
you have some function
that is called exactly

00:51:09.790 --> 00:51:13.430
that implements exactly
the operations that

00:51:13.430 --> 00:51:17.250
need to happen in order for
an answer to be generated.

00:51:17.250 --> 00:51:20.720
So you would have this
"find my teddy bear."

00:51:20.720 --> 00:51:25.640
And it would come with
some documentation,

00:51:25.640 --> 00:51:29.920
because the function
API that I showed you

00:51:29.920 --> 00:51:34.200
in the previous slides,
they typically-- so

00:51:34.200 --> 00:51:37.720
the way you would show the
LLM, the tools that you

00:51:37.720 --> 00:51:39.540
have at hand are exactly that.

00:51:39.540 --> 00:51:42.420
You would have the
API description,

00:51:42.420 --> 00:51:45.560
but you would also have the
inputs and the output shown

00:51:45.560 --> 00:51:49.200
to the LLM, which is why a
good documentation on what

00:51:49.200 --> 00:51:53.040
the function is meant
to do is important.

00:51:53.040 --> 00:51:57.800
And all the code logic
that you see in Python

00:51:57.800 --> 00:52:00.060
is not shown to the LLM.

00:52:00.060 --> 00:52:02.280
So these are things
that get executed

00:52:02.280 --> 00:52:05.100
once you choose that
tool to execute,

00:52:05.100 --> 00:52:07.100
but this is not
shown to the LLM.

00:52:07.100 --> 00:52:09.120
So these are
implementation details.

00:52:09.120 --> 00:52:13.360
And you might be able to do
anything that you want in there.

00:52:13.360 --> 00:52:15.640
So here, to find
teddy bears you need

00:52:15.640 --> 00:52:21.410
to query a backend with the
argument that is provided.

00:52:21.410 --> 00:52:25.450
And something else that is
important with tool calls

00:52:25.450 --> 00:52:29.610
is that you want your input
and output to be interpretable.

00:52:29.610 --> 00:52:31.250
So first, your inputs.

00:52:31.250 --> 00:52:34.930
You want your model to
be able to use your tool.

00:52:34.930 --> 00:52:39.830
So let's say you want it to
operate off of coordinates.

00:52:39.830 --> 00:52:43.310
So you need a clear
argument that says location,

00:52:43.310 --> 00:52:47.170
and you put the type
that it needs to put in.

00:52:47.170 --> 00:52:51.690
And in the same vibe
as it, the output

00:52:51.690 --> 00:52:53.970
needs to also be interpretable.

00:52:53.970 --> 00:52:57.250
Because once you
execute the tool call,

00:52:57.250 --> 00:53:00.570
then you need to feed whatever
the backend has returned back

00:53:00.570 --> 00:53:04.690
to the LLM and you want
it to interpret this back

00:53:04.690 --> 00:53:06.290
into natural language.

00:53:06.290 --> 00:53:09.010
So this is why you
see structured output

00:53:09.010 --> 00:53:14.490
types such as enums and the
likes being used in tool calls,

00:53:14.490 --> 00:53:18.690
because you want the
output to be structured.

00:53:18.690 --> 00:53:21.670
So here, for example, when
you want to find a teddy bear,

00:53:21.670 --> 00:53:26.070
it returns a class with an
argument for name, distance,

00:53:26.070 --> 00:53:29.130
mood, and so on.

00:53:29.130 --> 00:53:32.890
And these are, of
course, documented there.

00:53:32.890 --> 00:53:37.650
So all of that to say
that with these tools,

00:53:37.650 --> 00:53:41.610
you're able to return
an output that you

00:53:41.610 --> 00:53:43.670
can be happy with as a user.

00:53:43.670 --> 00:53:46.250
So here, we have
a teddy bear named

00:53:46.250 --> 00:53:53.290
Teddy that is near the user,
which is what the LLM mentions.

00:53:53.290 --> 00:53:59.650
And is everything clear so far?

00:53:59.650 --> 00:54:00.690
OK, awesome.

00:54:00.690 --> 00:54:05.090
So as you might suspect,
the world of tools

00:54:05.090 --> 00:54:09.950
is not only composed of tools to
find teddy bears and the likes,

00:54:09.950 --> 00:54:12.330
but it's way more
general than that.

00:54:12.330 --> 00:54:16.100
So one that you might have
interacted with quite a lot

00:54:16.100 --> 00:54:18.420
is web search.

00:54:18.420 --> 00:54:21.180
And usually, LLM
interfaces, they

00:54:21.180 --> 00:54:25.160
do a transparent job at showing
to you what they're doing.

00:54:25.160 --> 00:54:28.220
So if you see something
like searching the web,

00:54:28.220 --> 00:54:31.140
searching these websites,
something in the backend

00:54:31.140 --> 00:54:34.600
has predicted a search tool
call and is executing that.

00:54:34.600 --> 00:54:41.660
And these UI artifacts are here
for you to absorb the latency

00:54:41.660 --> 00:54:45.940
and have the LLM
process the results.

00:54:45.940 --> 00:54:50.500
But not only that, you
might imagine tool calls

00:54:50.500 --> 00:54:54.040
for anything else that you
might need a quick answer for.

00:54:54.040 --> 00:54:56.420
So let's say even though
you have access to the web,

00:54:56.420 --> 00:55:00.220
you might not want your
LLM to always use the web

00:55:00.220 --> 00:55:01.645
to search for anything else.

00:55:01.645 --> 00:55:03.520
So you might want
shortcuts for other things,

00:55:03.520 --> 00:55:07.980
for example, the weather,
stocks, and so on.

00:55:07.980 --> 00:55:11.080
And if you want to
query code bases,

00:55:11.080 --> 00:55:15.630
you might have a tool
that helps you do that.

00:55:15.630 --> 00:55:20.330
And another category of tool
might be computation based.

00:55:20.330 --> 00:55:23.310
So as you might know,
next token prediction

00:55:23.310 --> 00:55:27.290
is not great to do
computational math.

00:55:27.290 --> 00:55:29.270
So let's say if
you want to compute

00:55:29.270 --> 00:55:31.750
a product of two
numbers, you could have

00:55:31.750 --> 00:55:34.290
next-token prediction do it.

00:55:34.290 --> 00:55:36.750
And it's possible that
it could do it fine,

00:55:36.750 --> 00:55:38.350
but it doesn't do it great.

00:55:38.350 --> 00:55:42.510
So what's great is if you
convert all the numbers that you

00:55:42.510 --> 00:55:45.390
need to compute
into some tool call,

00:55:45.390 --> 00:55:49.670
you let the backend do
its job because it's

00:55:49.670 --> 00:55:52.270
a deterministic
operation and then

00:55:52.270 --> 00:55:55.670
you let a tool give you
back the results that you

00:55:55.670 --> 00:55:57.150
read to the user.

00:55:57.150 --> 00:56:01.510
So let's say if you want
to compute 2 times 3,

00:56:01.510 --> 00:56:07.310
you could have it both ways but
the calculator way is the most

00:56:07.310 --> 00:56:11.480
deterministic and efficient.

00:56:11.480 --> 00:56:12.620
Awesome.

00:56:12.620 --> 00:56:15.300
And another example
is code execution.

00:56:15.300 --> 00:56:19.580
So you mentioned, I think,
something more advanced.

00:56:19.580 --> 00:56:24.200
But if you ask for
something such as drawing

00:56:24.200 --> 00:56:27.600
a plot in your LLM
provider's text box,

00:56:27.600 --> 00:56:31.760
it might generate
a tool call that

00:56:31.760 --> 00:56:33.780
asks for the plot
to be generated

00:56:33.780 --> 00:56:35.380
and in that code
can be executed.

00:56:35.380 --> 00:56:39.700
And this is what you see as a
user drawn on your conversation.

00:56:39.700 --> 00:56:41.540
You might see plots
and the likes.

00:56:41.540 --> 00:56:45.360
So these are the results
of code execution.

00:56:45.360 --> 00:56:48.120
And then last, I
talked about actions.

00:56:48.120 --> 00:56:51.940
So you can also unlock
a whole range of things.

00:56:51.940 --> 00:56:54.200
So let's say you want
to send an email.

00:56:54.200 --> 00:56:59.680
You could think about tool
calls that take your intent,

00:56:59.680 --> 00:57:01.060
write your email.

00:57:01.060 --> 00:57:05.080
And also, they're able to
generate the action of sending

00:57:05.080 --> 00:57:06.880
the email on your behalf.

00:57:06.880 --> 00:57:11.890
So you could think all of
that in terms of tool calls.

00:57:11.890 --> 00:57:13.720
OK.

00:57:13.720 --> 00:57:16.890
So now-- yeah.

00:57:16.890 --> 00:57:19.530
So the question is
the set of tools

00:57:19.530 --> 00:57:23.270
that the model can use sets
at a given time in training?

00:57:23.270 --> 00:57:25.330
So we're going to see just now.

00:57:25.330 --> 00:57:26.830
And yeah, that's
a great question.

00:57:26.830 --> 00:57:31.650
So the answer is it
depends on how you proceed.

00:57:31.650 --> 00:57:35.790
So if you were to ask me the
question a year or two ago,

00:57:35.790 --> 00:57:39.690
I would have said yes, because
the practice back then was

00:57:39.690 --> 00:57:44.450
to decompose exactly what the
LLM needed to do in two stages

00:57:44.450 --> 00:57:48.890
and then do next-token
prediction on it as part of SFT.

00:57:48.890 --> 00:57:50.970
So you have two
stages there, one

00:57:50.970 --> 00:57:52.650
where you need to
predict the tool

00:57:52.650 --> 00:57:55.350
call itself and the arguments.

00:57:55.350 --> 00:57:58.130
So let's say find
a bear near me.

00:57:58.130 --> 00:58:01.850
You would have an example
that has this query and then

00:58:01.850 --> 00:58:05.570
all the APIs that we
have defined previously.

00:58:05.570 --> 00:58:09.540
And you would ask next-token
prediction to come up with

00:58:09.540 --> 00:58:14.380
the "find my position" first
tool call and the second tool

00:58:14.380 --> 00:58:17.260
call "find teddy bear
with this position."

00:58:17.260 --> 00:58:18.660
That's been found.

00:58:18.660 --> 00:58:20.580
So this was the case.

00:58:20.580 --> 00:58:25.140
And you would have a
second set of examples

00:58:25.140 --> 00:58:28.740
which are those that convert
those interpretable backend

00:58:28.740 --> 00:58:31.280
results back into
natural language

00:58:31.280 --> 00:58:35.220
so that third step where once
you have executed that tool

00:58:35.220 --> 00:58:38.180
call, you have the back end
that has returned something.

00:58:38.180 --> 00:58:42.340
And you want that something
to be read back to the user.

00:58:42.340 --> 00:58:45.260
So the answer was yes.

00:58:45.260 --> 00:58:50.140
But these days, no
longer the case.

00:58:50.140 --> 00:58:55.420
So the training pipeline that
we have seen a few lectures ago

00:58:55.420 --> 00:58:59.900
typically teaches the model
how to perform computations

00:58:59.900 --> 00:59:00.940
at large.

00:59:00.940 --> 00:59:05.580
So you might have some tool use
as part of the training process

00:59:05.580 --> 00:59:09.620
that shows how to use
Python in general,

00:59:09.620 --> 00:59:15.300
but then you would not bake that
as part of the model weights.

00:59:15.300 --> 00:59:17.980
You would use prompting instead.

00:59:17.980 --> 00:59:24.180
So here, when I mentioned
that we have a documentation

00:59:24.180 --> 00:59:27.900
string that comes
alongside the function API,

00:59:27.900 --> 00:59:32.260
this could be a place where
you could prompt your model

00:59:32.260 --> 00:59:34.560
to do actions in a certain way.

00:59:34.560 --> 00:59:41.040
So for example, let's say,
use this argument for this

00:59:41.040 --> 00:59:45.180
so typically as you would have
in regular Python docstrings.

00:59:45.180 --> 00:59:48.500
But this need is being
decreased more and more

00:59:48.500 --> 00:59:53.860
as time goes, because these
are all becoming native coding

00:59:53.860 --> 00:59:55.140
capabilities.

00:59:55.140 --> 00:59:57.540
So as long as your
tool is well-designed

00:59:57.540 --> 01:00:01.260
that the input and output
are interpretable, then

01:00:01.260 --> 01:00:06.470
I would say the need
goes down quite a bit.

01:00:06.470 --> 01:00:10.910
And so now that we have talked
about prompting and the need

01:00:10.910 --> 01:00:15.130
to write such descriptions
to train the model,

01:00:15.130 --> 01:00:18.390
I mean, train in the
wider sense, in the sense

01:00:18.390 --> 01:00:21.030
not to change
weights but to coach

01:00:21.030 --> 01:00:23.510
it to give the right answer.

01:00:23.510 --> 01:00:28.230
So does anyone have a
suggestion on how to write

01:00:28.230 --> 01:00:30.050
such a detailed explanation?

01:00:33.458 --> 01:00:35.750
Yeah, so the answer is use
a separate model to do that.

01:00:35.750 --> 01:00:37.870
So how?

01:00:37.870 --> 01:00:41.870
So the suggestion
is to give the API

01:00:41.870 --> 01:00:45.050
and then prompt the model to
just write the descriptions.

01:00:45.050 --> 01:00:47.230
So I think that would work.

01:00:47.230 --> 01:00:49.310
That is good.

01:00:49.310 --> 01:00:51.550
That would work.

01:00:51.550 --> 01:00:54.590
Does anyone have any concepts
more powerful than that, more

01:00:54.590 --> 01:00:55.490
grounded?

01:00:58.110 --> 01:00:59.990
Yeah.

01:00:59.990 --> 01:01:02.460
So the answer here is use MCP.

01:01:02.460 --> 01:01:04.620
So here, it would be
strictly orthogonal.

01:01:04.620 --> 01:01:06.420
So MCP would have
the same problem.

01:01:06.420 --> 01:01:10.440
So it's a way to
write your tool,

01:01:10.440 --> 01:01:12.560
but you would have
the same problem

01:01:12.560 --> 01:01:16.400
of what to prompt like what
prompt to put in your MCP

01:01:16.400 --> 01:01:19.040
descriptions like in your
tool descriptions and so on.

01:01:19.040 --> 01:01:23.080
So you write that they formalize
this concept of putting prompts

01:01:23.080 --> 01:01:26.480
here and there a bit, but they
do not give an answer to you

01:01:26.480 --> 01:01:29.200
in themselves.

01:01:29.200 --> 01:01:31.860
Yeah, so I think yeah
this was very close,

01:01:31.860 --> 01:01:35.000
so I'm going to be
eye opening here.

01:01:35.000 --> 01:01:37.760
So you could use what you want.

01:01:37.760 --> 01:01:40.200
So the training pairs that
we have seen in the past,

01:01:40.200 --> 01:01:47.160
you could use this as you being
able to tell what you want

01:01:47.160 --> 01:01:53.200
to a model and then let a
model iterate on descriptions

01:01:53.200 --> 01:01:57.640
until the resulting LLM can
generate the answer that you

01:01:57.640 --> 01:01:58.890
are looking for.

01:01:58.890 --> 01:02:03.210
So it's the same as you
said with the extension

01:02:03.210 --> 01:02:06.210
that you specifically know
what you're targeting,

01:02:06.210 --> 01:02:10.190
and you're steering model
prompt iteration towards it.

01:02:12.810 --> 01:02:16.770
So in practice, what you would
have is a bunch of queries

01:02:16.770 --> 01:02:20.610
that are of interest
to you and a set

01:02:20.610 --> 01:02:23.630
of labels, which are the
hardest to find in practice,

01:02:23.630 --> 01:02:27.410
because you need some
expert that writes

01:02:27.410 --> 01:02:28.690
the answers to all of these.

01:02:28.690 --> 01:02:31.390
You could generate them via LLM.

01:02:31.390 --> 01:02:33.150
You could have
synthetic outputs.

01:02:33.150 --> 01:02:38.070
But usually, they're not
great a lot of the time,

01:02:38.070 --> 01:02:39.970
because they are not
necessarily aligned

01:02:39.970 --> 01:02:41.730
with what you were looking for.

01:02:41.730 --> 01:02:45.530
So you need some work into
the labels themselves.

01:02:45.530 --> 01:02:49.290
And the trick here is
to generate the answers

01:02:49.290 --> 01:02:51.330
with the current
state of the prompt.

01:02:51.330 --> 01:02:55.470
So you would have this
actual column there.

01:02:55.470 --> 01:02:59.700
And then use another
LLM that sees

01:02:59.700 --> 01:03:03.860
both outputs and that iterates
on the prompts of the LLM

01:03:03.860 --> 01:03:08.100
that produced the actual
column in order to make it

01:03:08.100 --> 01:03:12.780
closer to the expected column.

01:03:12.780 --> 01:03:16.060
So does that make sense?

01:03:16.060 --> 01:03:19.500
OK, awesome.

01:03:19.500 --> 01:03:24.580
So we talked about
having multiple tools.

01:03:24.580 --> 01:03:29.820
The thing is you might have
more than just two or three.

01:03:29.820 --> 01:03:32.300
You might have a universe of it.

01:03:32.300 --> 01:03:34.400
Everyone is interested
in something.

01:03:34.400 --> 01:03:37.900
If you are an LLM provider,
your incentive at first

01:03:37.900 --> 01:03:41.300
is to provide as much
useful tools as possible

01:03:41.300 --> 01:03:42.780
for your users.

01:03:42.780 --> 01:03:45.580
Does anyone see a
problem with it.

01:03:45.580 --> 01:03:46.880
Yeah, the answer is conflict.

01:03:46.880 --> 01:03:47.540
Yeah, great.

01:03:47.540 --> 01:03:48.860
Spot on.

01:03:48.860 --> 01:03:53.600
So let's see what these
conflicts could be.

01:03:53.600 --> 01:03:58.410
So now, I'm going to anchor
this concept on an example.

01:03:58.410 --> 01:04:01.150
So show me my teddy
bear's morning routine.

01:04:01.150 --> 01:04:03.470
You could think that
it could be picked up

01:04:03.470 --> 01:04:07.230
by one of the tools
that is mentioned there.

01:04:07.230 --> 01:04:13.110
So let's say you have a
bunch of documents saved

01:04:13.110 --> 01:04:16.310
and some get_drive
tool retrieves

01:04:16.310 --> 01:04:20.510
the documents that is about your
teddy bear's morning routine.

01:04:20.510 --> 01:04:21.250
OK, great.

01:04:21.250 --> 01:04:22.750
You're happy with it.

01:04:22.750 --> 01:04:27.210
But it could be the case that,
exactly as you mentioned,

01:04:27.210 --> 01:04:29.190
there could be a
conflict in scope

01:04:29.190 --> 01:04:38.030
between that tool that picked
up this task with another tool.

01:04:38.030 --> 01:04:42.950
So let's say get_doc
could have an overlapping

01:04:42.950 --> 01:04:48.090
scope with get_drive and lead
you to a good response here.

01:04:48.090 --> 01:04:48.770
So we are happy.

01:04:48.770 --> 01:04:51.950
It's still a document
that we're retrieving.

01:04:51.950 --> 01:04:56.870
But still, it's an overlapping
capabilities that is exposed.

01:04:56.870 --> 01:05:00.830
And in some cases,
this is all right,

01:05:00.830 --> 01:05:05.270
because the outputs in
both cases are the same.

01:05:05.270 --> 01:05:10.830
So we say that the
conflict is benign.

01:05:10.830 --> 01:05:15.070
But in other cases,
it might change

01:05:15.070 --> 01:05:18.750
the interpretation of the query
that you have put as a user.

01:05:18.750 --> 01:05:22.190
So if you consider the query
"Can you find me my teddy bear?"

01:05:22.190 --> 01:05:24.430
you could have an
interpretation that

01:05:24.430 --> 01:05:27.750
could be one that looks
into your computer

01:05:27.750 --> 01:05:30.990
and then looks for some file
that matches this query.

01:05:30.990 --> 01:05:35.330
So you might have, I don't know,
a teddy bear picture somewhere

01:05:35.330 --> 01:05:37.750
and then your LLMs
thinks that it's

01:05:37.750 --> 01:05:41.470
helpful to show where
that picture is.

01:05:41.470 --> 01:05:44.390
But also, you could have
the interpretation that

01:05:44.390 --> 01:05:46.590
is the one that we
discussed just before,

01:05:46.590 --> 01:05:48.430
where you are
somewhere and you are

01:05:48.430 --> 01:05:54.880
asking where your teddy bear
is and the LLM would physically

01:05:54.880 --> 01:06:02.080
tell you where it is,
which is a problem here.

01:06:02.080 --> 01:06:07.920
So to mitigate this issue you
have a concept called tool

01:06:07.920 --> 01:06:12.160
selection that might-- yeah.

01:06:12.160 --> 01:06:16.360
So the comment here is,
is that really a problem,

01:06:16.360 --> 01:06:19.960
or is it more of a user
problem that they did not

01:06:19.960 --> 01:06:21.920
specify their query enough?

01:06:21.920 --> 01:06:23.600
Fair pushback.

01:06:23.600 --> 01:06:29.400
I think it might not be fair to
put that on the user in the case

01:06:29.400 --> 01:06:32.760
where there might be tools that
are active that the user doesn't

01:06:32.760 --> 01:06:35.960
care about that might
pick up the query instead.

01:06:35.960 --> 01:06:39.440
So I think there is a fine
balance between saying

01:06:39.440 --> 01:06:45.200
the query is underspecified
and, OK, it's something

01:06:45.200 --> 01:06:48.120
that is truly an issue.

01:06:48.120 --> 01:06:52.550
And typically, you would want
to err on the side of caution.

01:06:52.550 --> 01:06:55.770
If you see answers that do not
correspond to the actual user

01:06:55.770 --> 01:06:59.490
intents, it might be
a symptom that you

01:06:59.490 --> 01:07:04.010
might have gone a bit too far
in terms of number of tools.

01:07:04.010 --> 01:07:08.470
So yeah, so as a summary,
it's a good suggestion.

01:07:08.470 --> 01:07:10.330
But typically, the
people who control

01:07:10.330 --> 01:07:12.650
the space of tools and
the ones who serve them

01:07:12.650 --> 01:07:14.530
are two different entities.

01:07:14.530 --> 01:07:17.310
And in that sense,
it is a problem.

01:07:20.090 --> 01:07:22.410
Yeah.

01:07:22.410 --> 01:07:24.990
So let me know if I
understood the question right.

01:07:24.990 --> 01:07:29.490
So do we have the possibility
of enforcing determinism

01:07:29.490 --> 01:07:33.590
for this risk, like
conflict risk issues?

01:07:33.590 --> 01:07:35.450
Is that correct?

01:07:35.450 --> 01:07:37.070
So yeah, good point.

01:07:37.070 --> 01:07:40.250
So you could definitely think
of putting temperature to zero

01:07:40.250 --> 01:07:43.390
and maybe working
on determinism.

01:07:43.390 --> 01:07:47.540
But typically, when you do
that, the quality goes down.

01:07:47.540 --> 01:07:51.060
So determinism as
an enforced property

01:07:51.060 --> 01:07:54.900
might not be desirable here,
although it could help.

01:07:54.900 --> 01:08:01.540
So one other pushback one
might have is maybe determinism

01:08:01.540 --> 01:08:04.740
will lead that answer to
this underspecified question

01:08:04.740 --> 01:08:08.940
to always be wrong, so
it might not be also

01:08:08.940 --> 01:08:11.560
something that's desirable.

01:08:11.560 --> 01:08:12.060
Yeah.

01:08:16.340 --> 01:08:16.840
OK.

01:08:16.840 --> 01:08:18.819
Awesome.

01:08:18.819 --> 01:08:21.060
But we're going
to try to mitigate

01:08:21.060 --> 01:08:24.380
this issue with a concept
called tool selection.

01:08:24.380 --> 01:08:29.340
So instead of operating off of
the universe of tools, instead

01:08:29.340 --> 01:08:31.180
we take a subset.

01:08:31.180 --> 01:08:33.700
And in order to
mitigate the concern,

01:08:33.700 --> 01:08:36.180
we take some characteristics
here and there

01:08:36.180 --> 01:08:41.819
that can help us gauge what
tools would be better fed

01:08:41.819 --> 01:08:44.160
as a API functions.

01:08:44.160 --> 01:08:46.310
So I'm going to give
a few examples to you.

01:08:46.310 --> 01:08:48.870
So if, in your
conversation history,

01:08:48.870 --> 01:08:51.229
you're here talking about
the physical presence

01:08:51.229 --> 01:08:54.470
of teddy bears, then it
might be an indicator

01:08:54.470 --> 01:08:58.750
that fine teddy bear is the
most appropriate outcome.

01:08:58.750 --> 01:09:00.870
And then on memory
that we're going

01:09:00.870 --> 01:09:05.470
to talk about also later
on, if you have issued

01:09:05.470 --> 01:09:08.069
such queries in the past
and you have been more

01:09:08.069 --> 01:09:10.229
specific in your
queries, then it

01:09:10.229 --> 01:09:14.109
might mean that the query
that was underspecified

01:09:14.109 --> 01:09:16.350
should follow the same pattern.

01:09:16.350 --> 01:09:19.310
Similarly, from
the user profile,

01:09:19.310 --> 01:09:22.189
if you're operating in
an enterprise setting,

01:09:22.189 --> 01:09:24.830
you might have
different profiles

01:09:24.830 --> 01:09:27.890
associated to their own set
of tools that they use most.

01:09:27.890 --> 01:09:31.710
Let's say finance people, they
would trigger finance tools.

01:09:31.710 --> 01:09:34.290
Engineers, they would want
some other set of tools.

01:09:34.290 --> 01:09:39.790
So this could help
steer the tool kind.

01:09:39.790 --> 01:09:45.140
And then last, if
you have a LLM setup

01:09:45.140 --> 01:09:48.880
and you have users who can
activate or deactivate a given

01:09:48.880 --> 01:09:51.840
tool, then you might want
to take that preference

01:09:51.840 --> 01:09:52.700
into account.

01:09:52.700 --> 01:09:55.080
So you could well go
into the territory

01:09:55.080 --> 01:09:59.020
of suggesting to activate
every tool to your user.

01:09:59.020 --> 01:10:01.940
Let's say they have not
activated a certain tool

01:10:01.940 --> 01:10:04.840
but you do predict it, you
could have the user experience

01:10:04.840 --> 01:10:07.560
of asking them to
activate it, or you

01:10:07.560 --> 01:10:10.160
could err on the side
of caution and say,

01:10:10.160 --> 01:10:11.960
maybe this is not
what they wanted,

01:10:11.960 --> 01:10:14.840
and respect these settings.

01:10:14.840 --> 01:10:16.720
So you might have
some more, but I just

01:10:16.720 --> 01:10:20.000
want to say that tool
selection could be informed

01:10:20.000 --> 01:10:22.860
with all of these parameters.

01:10:22.860 --> 01:10:23.680
Yeah.

01:10:23.680 --> 01:10:24.620
Yeah, great question.

01:10:24.620 --> 01:10:27.960
So the question is how
complex is the routing model.

01:10:27.960 --> 01:10:30.920
So typically, it's a
super lightweight LLM,

01:10:30.920 --> 01:10:34.220
because you're still operating
in natural language space.

01:10:34.220 --> 01:10:36.520
So you need some
transformer-like architecture

01:10:36.520 --> 01:10:37.700
that works great.

01:10:37.700 --> 01:10:40.680
But exactly as you say,
it's not a very hard task.

01:10:40.680 --> 01:10:45.580
So you might not want to
have the most expensive model

01:10:45.580 --> 01:10:49.720
do that task and instead have
a lightweight one so typically

01:10:49.720 --> 01:10:53.040
a low latency.

01:10:53.040 --> 01:10:54.200
OK, great.

01:10:54.200 --> 01:10:58.940
So I want to say that tool
selection is a two-step process.

01:10:58.940 --> 01:11:02.360
So first, based on all
of these parameters,

01:11:02.360 --> 01:11:05.620
you select a subset of
tools that are of interest

01:11:05.620 --> 01:11:10.280
and these are those that you
put into the context window

01:11:10.280 --> 01:11:12.860
and ask the LLM to operate over.

01:11:15.640 --> 01:11:16.960
OK, great.

01:11:16.960 --> 01:11:18.860
Any questions on tool selection?

01:11:18.860 --> 01:11:19.660
Yeah.

01:11:19.660 --> 01:11:20.580
Yeah, great point.

01:11:20.580 --> 01:11:23.440
So the question is,
let's say two tools have

01:11:23.440 --> 01:11:25.220
overlapping capabilities.

01:11:25.220 --> 01:11:27.400
Is it the case that
we would have both

01:11:27.400 --> 01:11:29.680
of them selected in the router?

01:11:29.680 --> 01:11:32.820
So it all depends on prompting.

01:11:32.820 --> 01:11:34.360
So one thing that
I didn't say is

01:11:34.360 --> 01:11:36.600
that typically a
routing mechanism

01:11:36.600 --> 01:11:40.250
comes with also its
own set of prompting.

01:11:40.250 --> 01:11:42.530
So you might have
some rules that

01:11:42.530 --> 01:11:44.650
say if one tool
is selected, then

01:11:44.650 --> 01:11:46.230
you do not want the other one.

01:11:46.230 --> 01:11:50.450
So it's all at the
discretion of your use case.

01:11:50.450 --> 01:11:51.810
Yeah.

01:11:51.810 --> 01:11:56.970
So moving on, I'm going
to touch at one topic

01:11:56.970 --> 01:11:58.570
that Afshine has mentioned here.

01:11:58.570 --> 01:12:01.090
So even if we didn't have
this risk for conflicts

01:12:01.090 --> 01:12:04.370
and we had non-overlapping
tools everywhere

01:12:04.370 --> 01:12:09.170
and perfect prediction, another
reason why having too many tools

01:12:09.170 --> 01:12:13.410
is not appropriate is because
of this context rot phenomenon,

01:12:13.410 --> 01:12:17.330
where you would see a decrease
in performance in tool calling

01:12:17.330 --> 01:12:21.770
capabilities as the context
grows larger and larger.

01:12:21.770 --> 01:12:25.130
And you wouldn't want
the user to be burdened

01:12:25.130 --> 01:12:30.290
by so much context being used
as early as the first queries,

01:12:30.290 --> 01:12:33.090
which is why providers
are typically very mindful

01:12:33.090 --> 01:12:36.730
of what goes in the context.

01:12:36.730 --> 01:12:42.620
And yeah, minimizing
bloat is one of them.

01:12:42.620 --> 01:12:44.340
OK, awesome.

01:12:44.340 --> 01:12:48.140
And I'm going to touch on the
topic that you brought up.

01:12:48.140 --> 01:12:52.960
So I'm going to
motivate it first.

01:12:52.960 --> 01:12:56.020
So we have implemented
a tool, which is great.

01:12:56.020 --> 01:13:00.380
Now, let's suppose you have
several LLMs that you want

01:13:00.380 --> 01:13:03.220
to be able to use your tool.

01:13:03.220 --> 01:13:06.900
So if I didn't
have any standard,

01:13:06.900 --> 01:13:11.740
I would need to implement one
whole set of implementation

01:13:11.740 --> 01:13:14.180
for each LLM, because
they might each have

01:13:14.180 --> 01:13:17.700
their way of expressing things.

01:13:17.700 --> 01:13:20.300
A lot of the things that
I mentioned in the past

01:13:20.300 --> 01:13:24.680
are design questions, how
you write your tool API,

01:13:24.680 --> 01:13:26.820
how you do this and that.

01:13:26.820 --> 01:13:30.340
And in order to
mitigate that, you

01:13:30.340 --> 01:13:34.020
have standardization
protocols out there

01:13:34.020 --> 01:13:41.670
such as MCP that make your job
easy into writing out your tool.

01:13:41.670 --> 01:13:48.750
So it is a way to connect every
tool to every LLM provider that

01:13:48.750 --> 01:13:50.790
supports it.

01:13:50.790 --> 01:13:53.650
So yeah, who has heard of MCP?

01:13:53.650 --> 01:13:54.150
Yeah.

01:13:54.150 --> 01:13:54.650
OK.

01:13:54.650 --> 01:13:55.510
Everyone.

01:13:55.510 --> 01:13:59.430
So I'm going to be quite
brief, since everyone knows.

01:13:59.430 --> 01:14:02.570
So you have two concepts there.

01:14:02.570 --> 01:14:05.950
You have one that's called
the MCP server, which

01:14:05.950 --> 01:14:08.390
is going to be the one
that contains your tool

01:14:08.390 --> 01:14:10.470
implementation and
every business logic

01:14:10.470 --> 01:14:12.470
that you want to bring to it.

01:14:12.470 --> 01:14:15.590
And the partner to
MCP server is going

01:14:15.590 --> 01:14:22.370
to be MCP clients, which will be
ones that will query your server

01:14:22.370 --> 01:14:25.310
and they will be interested
in using your tools.

01:14:25.310 --> 01:14:29.910
So you have these two concepts
so MCP server and MCP client.

01:14:29.910 --> 01:14:34.220
You, as a user, you do not
see the MCP server directly.

01:14:34.220 --> 01:14:36.640
You interact with the
client, and then the client

01:14:36.640 --> 01:14:41.160
can call the MCP server
for things like tool calls

01:14:41.160 --> 01:14:42.680
and so on.

01:14:42.680 --> 01:14:45.480
And the magic behind
MCP is that it

01:14:45.480 --> 01:14:49.600
standardizes how a tool and
so on should be defined.

01:14:49.600 --> 01:14:52.360
So it defines
three buckets, one,

01:14:52.360 --> 01:14:56.860
which is tools, which is
exactly what we discussed here.

01:14:56.860 --> 01:14:58.920
So it gives some
standardized templates

01:14:58.920 --> 01:15:04.480
to indicate tool arguments,
outputs, and so on.

01:15:04.480 --> 01:15:06.960
And there is a
concept of prompts,

01:15:06.960 --> 01:15:14.560
which are handles and shortcuts
into specific workflows that

01:15:14.560 --> 01:15:16.080
involve your tools.

01:15:16.080 --> 01:15:19.880
And you have resources
that might be things

01:15:19.880 --> 01:15:22.640
that your application
layers-- so typically your MCP

01:15:22.640 --> 01:15:27.480
clients-- might want to
feed your tools and so on.

01:15:27.480 --> 01:15:31.300
So if I were to take the
example of a teddy bear

01:15:31.300 --> 01:15:33.970
that's very
interesting in poetry.

01:15:33.970 --> 01:15:37.410
If you are interested in
answering a recommendation

01:15:37.410 --> 01:15:39.690
question based on
an MCP server, you

01:15:39.690 --> 01:15:42.890
could think of two
tools, one that

01:15:42.890 --> 01:15:47.130
is to find a given poetry
book and another one that is

01:15:47.130 --> 01:15:49.370
to recommend books in general.

01:15:49.370 --> 01:15:51.330
You could think
about prompts that

01:15:51.330 --> 01:15:55.090
would be one that finds a
specific title and another one

01:15:55.090 --> 01:16:00.610
that is to recommend books
based on a teddy bear's taste.

01:16:00.610 --> 01:16:03.770
And then finally, some
resources that you

01:16:03.770 --> 01:16:07.850
might want to make available
to your application layer

01:16:07.850 --> 01:16:11.890
for the user to provide
could be a teddy bear's

01:16:11.890 --> 01:16:14.070
personal collection
of existing books

01:16:14.070 --> 01:16:18.290
and so on for the tools to get
a gist of what the teddy bear is

01:16:18.290 --> 01:16:19.550
after.

01:16:19.550 --> 01:16:20.050
Yeah.

01:16:20.050 --> 01:16:22.050
So it's a way to
standardize all this

01:16:22.050 --> 01:16:27.330
and put it into buckets that
are standardized across tools.

01:16:27.330 --> 01:16:29.830
So that once you implement
all of these once,

01:16:29.830 --> 01:16:33.610
you can connect it anywhere.

01:16:33.610 --> 01:16:36.250
OK, great.

01:16:36.250 --> 01:16:39.670
And I'm going to touch
on another point.

01:16:39.670 --> 01:16:43.810
So no one made any
pushback to me saying,

01:16:43.810 --> 01:16:46.130
OK, let's just write prompts.

01:16:46.130 --> 01:16:48.850
Yeah, so I'm going
to push back myself.

01:16:48.850 --> 01:16:49.830
What about the cost?

01:16:49.830 --> 01:16:53.050
We're going to prompt
all of these instructions

01:16:53.050 --> 01:16:55.230
to how to use tools
into the prompt.

01:16:55.230 --> 01:16:59.610
But doesn't that result
in expensive bill?

01:16:59.610 --> 01:17:00.710
So I'm asking you.

01:17:03.730 --> 01:17:06.638
Yeah.

01:17:06.638 --> 01:17:08.390
Yeah, the answer here
is prompt caching.

01:17:08.390 --> 01:17:09.030
Yes, spot on.

01:17:09.030 --> 01:17:10.950
Yeah, exactly right.

01:17:10.950 --> 01:17:14.570
So I'm going to show a
similar set of screenshots

01:17:14.570 --> 01:17:16.350
as what Afshine mentioned.

01:17:16.350 --> 01:17:18.430
And as you can see
across providers,

01:17:18.430 --> 01:17:21.690
you have a range of cost
savings with respect

01:17:21.690 --> 01:17:26.370
to regular inputs, tokens that
range in the order of magnitude

01:17:26.370 --> 01:17:28.180
of O of 10.

01:17:28.180 --> 01:17:30.020
And there is another
topic that we

01:17:30.020 --> 01:17:32.520
haven't talked about just yet.

01:17:32.520 --> 01:17:35.900
So these days, you start
seeing more columns

01:17:35.900 --> 01:17:40.740
about caching that is
about how long the cache is

01:17:40.740 --> 01:17:42.760
being held in the cache.

01:17:42.760 --> 01:17:46.780
So five minutes writes an
hour write, as you see there.

01:17:46.780 --> 01:17:50.140
So the first time the
prompt will be issued,

01:17:50.140 --> 01:17:51.620
it will be cached.

01:17:51.620 --> 01:17:55.620
And depending on the
solution that you selected,

01:17:55.620 --> 01:17:59.780
then that duration will be the
time to leave of that cache.

01:17:59.780 --> 01:18:04.660
So you take an upfront
bill on caching these.

01:18:04.660 --> 01:18:07.500
But then if, under
the time to leave,

01:18:07.500 --> 01:18:14.020
you re-request these cached
inputs, then you can reuse it.

01:18:14.020 --> 01:18:18.500
So let's say you have
a high-volume workflow

01:18:18.500 --> 01:18:23.920
and then you have a cached input
that is reused all the time,

01:18:23.920 --> 01:18:28.070
every minute then selecting
five minutes time to leave

01:18:28.070 --> 01:18:31.790
is a great fit because you
pay the price to store it once

01:18:31.790 --> 01:18:36.270
and then it's reused afterwards.

01:18:36.270 --> 01:18:37.870
Yeah.

01:18:37.870 --> 01:18:39.590
OK, great.

01:18:39.590 --> 01:18:42.650
Now, we're going to do
an exercise together.

01:18:42.650 --> 01:18:45.470
So let's get slightly
more concrete.

01:18:45.470 --> 01:18:49.630
So we have in our
prompt the user prompt

01:18:49.630 --> 01:18:52.950
that is the query
that you issue,

01:18:52.950 --> 01:18:56.430
the tool definitions that
are the ones that either

01:18:56.430 --> 01:18:59.010
have been selected by the
tool selection process

01:18:59.010 --> 01:19:02.510
or are those that are
contained in the tools

01:19:02.510 --> 01:19:05.870
that we want to serve you that
are already in the context,

01:19:05.870 --> 01:19:10.030
and you have some other
things like the user context.

01:19:10.030 --> 01:19:13.510
So if you look at
open-source implementations,

01:19:13.510 --> 01:19:15.550
oftentimes you
have the same words

01:19:15.550 --> 01:19:19.410
that ground your query
into your environment.

01:19:19.410 --> 01:19:23.390
So typically, you have the
time and date and so on.

01:19:23.390 --> 01:19:25.520
And I have a question for you.

01:19:25.520 --> 01:19:33.820
Is this ordering of messages
one that you would agree with?

01:19:33.820 --> 01:19:34.320
Yeah.

01:19:34.320 --> 01:19:36.420
So the question is,
what is user context?

01:19:36.420 --> 01:19:41.280
User context is not
written by the user.

01:19:41.280 --> 01:19:44.360
These are things that are
inferred by the LLM provider,

01:19:44.360 --> 01:19:48.040
and they will stuff
it into your prompt.

01:19:48.040 --> 01:19:55.240
You will not see it, but it
will help the LLM make up

01:19:55.240 --> 01:19:56.180
for better answers.

01:19:56.180 --> 01:19:59.640
So let's say if we are
in November, you're like,

01:19:59.640 --> 01:20:00.540
what day is it?

01:20:00.540 --> 01:20:01.940
You don't need a tool for that.

01:20:01.940 --> 01:20:04.160
It will just read
off the user context.

01:20:04.160 --> 01:20:07.560
Similarly, your rough location
given by your IP address

01:20:07.560 --> 01:20:10.640
might help steer web searches
towards the ones that

01:20:10.640 --> 01:20:13.080
are relevant to you.

01:20:13.080 --> 01:20:14.000
Yeah, spot on.

01:20:14.000 --> 01:20:16.900
So the question is,
does user context,

01:20:16.900 --> 01:20:20.220
is it something that
happens at every query?

01:20:20.220 --> 01:20:22.970
So as you see, there
is a time component.

01:20:22.970 --> 01:20:30.070
So here, it's about 5:55 And
the query includes the time.

01:20:30.070 --> 01:20:32.650
So every time you issue a
new query, it will change.

01:20:32.650 --> 01:20:36.610
So suppose that user context
changes, user prompt, of course,

01:20:36.610 --> 01:20:40.810
changes, because you issue
prompt of several kinds.

01:20:40.810 --> 01:20:43.130
And what about tool definitions?

01:20:43.130 --> 01:20:45.000
Does it change.

01:20:45.000 --> 01:20:45.750
Yeah, great point.

01:20:45.750 --> 01:20:47.690
So yes, because
it's selected tools.

01:20:47.690 --> 01:20:53.290
Suppose these are not selected,
you have a fixed set of tools?

01:20:53.290 --> 01:20:53.970
Yeah.

01:20:53.970 --> 01:20:54.270
OK.

01:20:54.270 --> 01:20:55.103
It's the same, yeah.

01:20:55.103 --> 01:20:57.650
And if it's those
that are selected,

01:20:57.650 --> 01:20:59.610
one could argue they
could be considered

01:20:59.610 --> 01:21:03.650
fixed across a subset of prompts
that select the same tools.

01:21:03.650 --> 01:21:05.510
So yeah, you have a great point.

01:21:05.510 --> 01:21:09.370
And the same conclusion
could roughly apply.

01:21:09.370 --> 01:21:13.010
So do you think that
this ordering of messages

01:21:13.010 --> 01:21:14.910
is a correct one?

01:21:14.910 --> 01:21:15.410
Yeah.

01:21:15.410 --> 01:21:19.690
So the answer here is that the
fixed part is not at the top,

01:21:19.690 --> 01:21:22.630
so you cannot leverage
prompt caching.

01:21:22.630 --> 01:21:27.470
So you have aced my little game.

01:21:27.470 --> 01:21:28.750
So yes, that's correct.

01:21:28.750 --> 01:21:31.610
You need to put the fixed
part at the top in order

01:21:31.610 --> 01:21:34.050
to leverage prompt caching.

01:21:34.050 --> 01:21:35.830
Is that obvious to everyone?

01:21:40.370 --> 01:21:43.750
Because once you put the
fixed part at the top,

01:21:43.750 --> 01:21:46.870
then the next time you
issue a different prompt,

01:21:46.870 --> 01:21:49.610
you will have variable
parts that will change.

01:21:49.610 --> 01:21:52.530
But this set of fixed
instructions that

01:21:52.530 --> 01:21:55.970
are the same across
prompts will be reused

01:21:55.970 --> 01:21:57.770
and you will hit
the cache there.

01:21:57.770 --> 01:22:01.730
So everything that I
mentioned regarding

01:22:01.730 --> 01:22:05.807
tuning of instructions,
they will be just fine.

01:22:05.807 --> 01:22:07.390
They will not cost
you a lot of money.

01:22:07.390 --> 01:22:09.070
They will cost cached money.

01:22:12.410 --> 01:22:14.250
OK.

01:22:14.250 --> 01:22:16.410
Yeah.

01:22:16.410 --> 01:22:18.960
So the question is, when
do you use a caching?

01:22:18.960 --> 01:22:21.000
Are you caching keys and values?

01:22:21.000 --> 01:22:22.420
Yes.

01:22:22.420 --> 01:22:25.500
So the question is, let's
say you change your tool.

01:22:25.500 --> 01:22:29.040
Is it the case that you
need to cache it once again?

01:22:29.040 --> 01:22:30.280
Yes, the caching is gone.

01:22:30.280 --> 01:22:34.140
You need to reheat the
cache, which is no big deal.

01:22:34.140 --> 01:22:37.460
Because typically, you would
change your tool descriptions,

01:22:37.460 --> 01:22:41.140
let's say, at most once a day
and ship it to production.

01:22:41.140 --> 01:22:42.940
But here, we're
talking about requests

01:22:42.940 --> 01:22:45.140
that you might
receive all at once,

01:22:45.140 --> 01:22:47.820
hundreds of requests
every minute.

01:22:47.820 --> 01:22:50.360
So yeah, it's a
small cost to pay.

01:22:53.660 --> 01:22:54.320
OK, great.

01:22:54.320 --> 01:22:57.420
So we took some time
to cover tool calling,

01:22:57.420 --> 01:23:02.940
because it's the foundation
of agentic systems.

01:23:02.940 --> 01:23:04.740
And now, we're going
to see everything

01:23:04.740 --> 01:23:06.680
that comes to the top of it.

01:23:06.680 --> 01:23:12.060
So you might have heard of
this concept of harness.

01:23:12.060 --> 01:23:14.540
Who has heard of harness?

01:23:14.540 --> 01:23:15.440
OK, everyone.

01:23:15.440 --> 01:23:15.940
Awesome.

01:23:15.940 --> 01:23:19.510
So it's the trendy keyword
of 2026 so makes sense.

01:23:19.510 --> 01:23:24.190
So I want to attempt at
a definition of harness

01:23:24.190 --> 01:23:25.650
based on intuition.

01:23:25.650 --> 01:23:29.150
So you have the model that
powers your LLM system.

01:23:29.150 --> 01:23:30.830
And the harness is
the term that we

01:23:30.830 --> 01:23:37.550
put to denote everything that
is surrounding it but the model.

01:23:37.550 --> 01:23:40.830
So it includes tools
that we covered.

01:23:40.830 --> 01:23:45.950
It covers how actions
should be ordered.

01:23:45.950 --> 01:23:50.430
It's things like compaction
strategy, memory, anything

01:23:50.430 --> 01:23:52.330
about prompting and so on.

01:23:54.990 --> 01:23:56.710
So we covered tools.

01:23:56.710 --> 01:23:59.190
And an analogy
that I want to give

01:23:59.190 --> 01:24:03.350
on how you should see model and
harness interacting with one

01:24:03.350 --> 01:24:07.370
another is that they are
both complementary pieces.

01:24:07.370 --> 01:24:11.750
And you cannot have a weak model
with a strong harness and hope

01:24:11.750 --> 01:24:15.200
for the best, nor the opposite.

01:24:15.200 --> 01:24:18.120
So here, you could
think about the model

01:24:18.120 --> 01:24:21.780
as being an engine and a
harness, everything around it.

01:24:21.780 --> 01:24:24.700
And if you want to have a car
that you can drive everywhere,

01:24:24.700 --> 01:24:27.920
you need both.

01:24:27.920 --> 01:24:32.840
So now, I want to
introduce a paradigm that

01:24:32.840 --> 01:24:35.800
was formalized in
2023 that helps

01:24:35.800 --> 01:24:39.280
to put words into practices
that a lot of people

01:24:39.280 --> 01:24:41.320
do in their own way.

01:24:41.320 --> 01:24:44.560
So ReAct, which is the
combination of reason

01:24:44.560 --> 01:24:49.400
plus act, which defines
the succession of actions

01:24:49.400 --> 01:24:54.400
and thoughts that a model can
have in order to reach a goal.

01:24:54.400 --> 01:24:57.900
So the flow is as follows.

01:24:57.900 --> 01:24:59.560
You have a given input.

01:24:59.560 --> 01:25:03.280
And the first
intuition of the model

01:25:03.280 --> 01:25:06.280
will be to observe
what's the current state.

01:25:06.280 --> 01:25:08.880
Then it will plan
for any actions

01:25:08.880 --> 01:25:11.760
it needs to perform
and then execute

01:25:11.760 --> 01:25:14.810
those actions in
the step called act

01:25:14.810 --> 01:25:18.570
and then observe what the
actions have let the system

01:25:18.570 --> 01:25:20.170
to be.

01:25:20.170 --> 01:25:23.690
And you might do this
loop any number of time

01:25:23.690 --> 01:25:26.210
until the LLM
system is satisfied

01:25:26.210 --> 01:25:29.410
in an observed state,
which is when you give

01:25:29.410 --> 01:25:32.410
an output back to the user.

01:25:32.410 --> 01:25:35.570
So I'm going to walk you
through all these steps based

01:25:35.570 --> 01:25:39.130
on an example just to make
sure if it makes sense.

01:25:39.130 --> 01:25:43.710
So we're in November.

01:25:43.710 --> 01:25:44.670
It's still hot outside.

01:25:44.670 --> 01:25:45.990
Let's say it's in two months.

01:25:45.990 --> 01:25:47.210
It's getting cold.

01:25:47.210 --> 01:25:48.930
And your teddy bear is cold.

01:25:48.930 --> 01:25:52.370
Your teddy bear is complaining
and wants you to do something.

01:25:52.370 --> 01:25:56.010
So you put it as an
input to your agent.

01:25:56.010 --> 01:25:58.810
The observed state
could be to notice

01:25:58.810 --> 01:26:00.190
that the teddy bear is cold.

01:26:00.190 --> 01:26:03.730
Hence, it has something
to do with temperature.

01:26:03.730 --> 01:26:09.330
And the first
reflex of the agent

01:26:09.330 --> 01:26:12.880
could be to first ground
the current temperature.

01:26:12.880 --> 01:26:17.860
So the plan step could
be to try to just learn

01:26:17.860 --> 01:26:19.980
about the current temperature.

01:26:19.980 --> 01:26:23.740
And then the act step
could then be the execution

01:26:23.740 --> 01:26:26.860
of a function that
evaluates temperature

01:26:26.860 --> 01:26:29.860
and read it back to the LLM.

01:26:29.860 --> 01:26:31.680
And now, we know
the temperature.

01:26:31.680 --> 01:26:35.980
We know what temperatures are
comfortable for a teddy bear.

01:26:35.980 --> 01:26:38.560
And we see that we are
below that temperature,

01:26:38.560 --> 01:26:42.300
so we want to plan ahead
and plan for an increase

01:26:42.300 --> 01:26:48.980
in temperature, which tells the
model to execute a tool called

01:26:48.980 --> 01:26:52.180
"increase temperature"
and so on.

01:26:52.180 --> 01:26:55.620
And at the end, once you have
increased the temperature

01:26:55.620 --> 01:27:01.100
and verified that the
temperature is now

01:27:01.100 --> 01:27:05.160
giving the air temperature
at a reasonable one,

01:27:05.160 --> 01:27:07.860
then you're good.

01:27:07.860 --> 01:27:11.060
So what I described
to you is the journey

01:27:11.060 --> 01:27:16.060
of an agent that's focused
on adjusting temperatures so

01:27:16.060 --> 01:27:17.980
like a thermostat agent.

01:27:17.980 --> 01:27:21.780
You could think about agents
all around you that similarly

01:27:21.780 --> 01:27:24.820
have an LLM and are
powered by tools

01:27:24.820 --> 01:27:27.340
and can do their own things.

01:27:27.340 --> 01:27:32.165
And these LLMs
powered by tools might

01:27:32.165 --> 01:27:34.040
be interested in
interacting with each other.

01:27:34.040 --> 01:27:35.040
Did you have a question?

01:27:35.040 --> 01:27:35.860
Yeah.

01:27:35.860 --> 01:27:39.120
So the question is,
observe, plan and act,

01:27:39.120 --> 01:27:42.580
are these steps that
are learned through SFT?

01:27:42.580 --> 01:27:44.100
And the answer is yes.

01:27:44.100 --> 01:27:46.020
Largely as part of
training, the model

01:27:46.020 --> 01:27:47.780
will learn how to use tools.

01:27:47.780 --> 01:27:53.580
So all of these will become
native capabilities of the LLM.

01:27:53.580 --> 01:27:56.820
So the question is
there some reasoning

01:27:56.820 --> 01:28:00.800
baked into that paradigm?

01:28:00.800 --> 01:28:01.840
So the answer is yes.

01:28:01.840 --> 01:28:03.740
So every time you
execute a tool,

01:28:03.740 --> 01:28:08.950
you need to have a dedicated
stage that recaps what happened.

01:28:08.950 --> 01:28:11.670
So in a sense, I would say
it's needed reasoning and not

01:28:11.670 --> 01:28:14.070
necessarily the same
as the one that we

01:28:14.070 --> 01:28:15.670
have seen in reasoning
models, which

01:28:15.670 --> 01:28:17.730
is an extra layer of reasoning.

01:28:17.730 --> 01:28:20.830
So you might think of
these three steps happening

01:28:20.830 --> 01:28:22.750
in even non-reasoning models.

01:28:22.750 --> 01:28:24.050
These are needed steps.

01:28:27.270 --> 01:28:27.770
OK, great.

01:28:27.770 --> 01:28:30.050
Any other questions?

01:28:30.050 --> 01:28:30.550
OK.

01:28:30.550 --> 01:28:34.050
So I'm going to go
slightly faster here.

01:28:34.050 --> 01:28:40.470
So similar to tools, agents
have their own protocol

01:28:40.470 --> 01:28:42.050
to interact with each other.

01:28:42.050 --> 01:28:44.550
So you might see
standardization methods

01:28:44.550 --> 01:28:47.330
that make them speak
the same language.

01:28:47.330 --> 01:28:50.030
So you might have
heard of A2A, which

01:28:50.030 --> 01:28:55.950
was released last year, that
defines the set of skills

01:28:55.950 --> 01:28:58.430
that a given agent
can do and define

01:28:58.430 --> 01:29:03.270
some functions that other
agents can interact with.

01:29:03.270 --> 01:29:08.780
And this brings me to another
aspect of these agents.

01:29:08.780 --> 01:29:13.120
So we talked about
executing actions and so on.

01:29:13.120 --> 01:29:16.800
There is a point in time where
you will reach the context

01:29:16.800 --> 01:29:19.880
window, and you have no
choice but to do something

01:29:19.880 --> 01:29:24.000
about it because
nothing can go after it.

01:29:24.000 --> 01:29:28.000
So I'm going to discuss
compaction strategies.

01:29:28.000 --> 01:29:34.240
So there is one where when
you do all these tool calls,

01:29:34.240 --> 01:29:36.840
you generate tool
outputs that are only

01:29:36.840 --> 01:29:39.120
needed at the following turn.

01:29:39.120 --> 01:29:42.400
So you might have an
opportunity to save

01:29:42.400 --> 01:29:44.840
on tokens that
were generated then

01:29:44.840 --> 01:29:49.160
and remove some of that
no longer needed piece

01:29:49.160 --> 01:29:53.520
of information, which is why
deleting stuff in your context

01:29:53.520 --> 01:29:56.600
window to make it shorter
could be one approach.

01:29:56.600 --> 01:29:58.040
You have a second
approach, which

01:29:58.040 --> 01:30:02.400
could be strictly time based
that says everything that I've

01:30:02.400 --> 01:30:08.370
talked so far is as relevant
as the time distance to which I

01:30:08.370 --> 01:30:12.130
discussed with my LLM, which
is why a sliding window might

01:30:12.130 --> 01:30:15.770
be one that makes sense.

01:30:15.770 --> 01:30:18.370
And you have a last
strategy that is one

01:30:18.370 --> 01:30:21.010
that I would say is
the most popular.

01:30:21.010 --> 01:30:23.910
So first of all, you could use
all of these in combination.

01:30:23.910 --> 01:30:25.850
But there is one
that is quite popular

01:30:25.850 --> 01:30:28.330
is to structure everything
that you have done

01:30:28.330 --> 01:30:33.570
so far into predefined buckets
and then have some summarization

01:30:33.570 --> 01:30:37.090
process go on and
continue the conversation

01:30:37.090 --> 01:30:40.650
with these small
summary and whatever

01:30:40.650 --> 01:30:43.170
you want to input afterwards.

01:30:43.170 --> 01:30:46.410
So here, I'm giving the
example of Pi, the coding

01:30:46.410 --> 01:30:48.930
agent for those
who have heard it

01:30:48.930 --> 01:30:53.250
that defines these current
progress, remaining work and so

01:30:53.250 --> 01:31:00.490
on, buckets that you can infer
with this compaction strategy.

01:31:00.490 --> 01:31:05.480
So now, we have talked
about compaction,

01:31:05.480 --> 01:31:08.700
which is at the
conversation level,

01:31:08.700 --> 01:31:12.220
maybe you want information to
persist for longer than that.

01:31:12.220 --> 01:31:14.300
So we're going to
talk about memory.

01:31:14.300 --> 01:31:16.580
First of all, I
want to introduce

01:31:16.580 --> 01:31:19.940
this terminology of
short-term memory, which

01:31:19.940 --> 01:31:21.260
you might have heard.

01:31:21.260 --> 01:31:26.820
So this is just a fancy term
to denote the context window.

01:31:26.820 --> 01:31:29.020
So it's what you
have talked about so

01:31:29.020 --> 01:31:32.860
far and might be retrievable.

01:31:32.860 --> 01:31:38.820
And you have a longer
term concept of memory

01:31:38.820 --> 01:31:40.780
that is called
long-term memory, where

01:31:40.780 --> 01:31:43.100
you talk about
things to your LLM

01:31:43.100 --> 01:31:45.860
and you might want
it to remember.

01:31:45.860 --> 01:31:51.980
So for example, let's say at
some point you brainstormed

01:31:51.980 --> 01:31:54.180
things you could buy as a gift.

01:31:54.180 --> 01:31:58.840
And you had some keywords
that said to the LLM, OK.

01:31:58.840 --> 01:32:01.030
Hey, I need to remember this.

01:32:01.030 --> 01:32:03.750
And this is a piece
of information

01:32:03.750 --> 01:32:06.070
that you want to reuse later on.

01:32:06.070 --> 01:32:09.830
So connecting it to what Afshine
was mentioning at the beginning,

01:32:09.830 --> 01:32:14.270
you could perform RAG
based on past conversations

01:32:14.270 --> 01:32:16.950
in order to retrieve
things of interest that

01:32:16.950 --> 01:32:20.630
are aligned with what the user
is talking at a given point.

01:32:20.630 --> 01:32:24.510
Or you could do a similarly
structured approach

01:32:24.510 --> 01:32:27.650
as what the summarization
for compaction did.

01:32:27.650 --> 01:32:31.350
You could have some
tool waiting for cues

01:32:31.350 --> 01:32:35.990
that ask for memory to be
saved and execute a memory tool

01:32:35.990 --> 01:32:39.790
and have a piece of
information stored somewhere.

01:32:39.790 --> 01:32:42.350
And that somewhere
could be something

01:32:42.350 --> 01:32:45.770
that exists as part of your
context window at all times.

01:32:48.430 --> 01:32:51.910
So this was memory.

01:32:51.910 --> 01:32:53.670
Yeah.

01:32:53.670 --> 01:32:56.790
So is the question, if you
have some reasoning traces

01:32:56.790 --> 01:32:59.150
as part of the LLM
response, is that something

01:32:59.150 --> 01:33:02.310
that you want to be
kept after compaction?

01:33:02.310 --> 01:33:07.150
So it depends on if it
contributes to each of the axes

01:33:07.150 --> 01:33:08.870
that you're interested in.

01:33:08.870 --> 01:33:10.570
But your gut feeling is right.

01:33:10.570 --> 01:33:14.570
I do not think that would be
the majority of what remains.

01:33:14.570 --> 01:33:16.590
It's mostly a very
high-level view

01:33:16.590 --> 01:33:20.210
of what the user was
interested in so far.

01:33:20.210 --> 01:33:21.790
So you wouldn't see
a reasoning trace

01:33:21.790 --> 01:33:25.710
as is, but more so of a
very high-level description

01:33:25.710 --> 01:33:30.590
of the gist of what
the user wanted to do.

01:33:30.590 --> 01:33:33.070
Yeah.

01:33:33.070 --> 01:33:33.810
OK, great.

01:33:33.810 --> 01:33:38.550
So now, moving on to an
application that all of you

01:33:38.550 --> 01:33:41.230
are big fans of I'm sure.

01:33:41.230 --> 01:33:46.910
Coding agents, they rely on
this concept of reason plus act

01:33:46.910 --> 01:33:47.650
at its core.

01:33:47.650 --> 01:33:50.630
So the thing that you see
in the middle agent loop

01:33:50.630 --> 01:33:56.560
is the succession of tool calls
and then observing what happens.

01:33:56.560 --> 01:34:01.680
And some main specificities
of coding agents

01:34:01.680 --> 01:34:04.040
is that they interact
with your system.

01:34:04.040 --> 01:34:06.920
So you have this
execution environment

01:34:06.920 --> 01:34:11.280
that is deeply characteristic
of coding agents.

01:34:11.280 --> 01:34:14.840
And I want to talk about
a few concepts there.

01:34:14.840 --> 01:34:20.640
So first, when you ask for a
given query in a coding agent,

01:34:20.640 --> 01:34:22.880
you have this problem
that doesn't necessarily

01:34:22.880 --> 01:34:25.200
occur in regular LLM
interactions, which

01:34:25.200 --> 01:34:27.560
is one where you
want to make sure

01:34:27.560 --> 01:34:32.080
that the action that the LLM
wants to perform is safe.

01:34:32.080 --> 01:34:36.600
So you might have seen
this approval mechanism

01:34:36.600 --> 01:34:38.120
about a year ago.

01:34:38.120 --> 01:34:41.280
Everyone had to
manually say allow

01:34:41.280 --> 01:34:44.760
or don't allow at every actions.

01:34:44.760 --> 01:34:47.040
That is largely no
longer the case.

01:34:47.040 --> 01:34:49.960
These days, you have
some lighter model

01:34:49.960 --> 01:34:53.850
that looks at everything
so far and that judges

01:34:53.850 --> 01:34:57.530
of the safety of the proposed
action independently.

01:34:57.530 --> 01:35:00.450
So you have this concept
of auto-approval.

01:35:00.450 --> 01:35:03.670
And something else that
is quite interesting.

01:35:03.670 --> 01:35:05.530
So Afshine talked about
RAG at the beginning

01:35:05.530 --> 01:35:09.810
of the lecture and grep
as a coding-friendly way

01:35:09.810 --> 01:35:12.390
to retrieve information.

01:35:12.390 --> 01:35:15.210
You will see that coding
agent, they largely

01:35:15.210 --> 01:35:18.490
ignore RAG, like
the traditional RAG,

01:35:18.490 --> 01:35:23.690
because you will see
regexes being incredibly

01:35:23.690 --> 01:35:26.130
predictive into
what the user wants,

01:35:26.130 --> 01:35:28.070
as opposed to semantic meaning.

01:35:28.070 --> 01:35:33.290
So let's say if you're asking
for something specific,

01:35:33.290 --> 01:35:37.730
then the agent guessing some
variations of what you want

01:35:37.730 --> 01:35:41.630
and searching for them
via grep is so efficient.

01:35:41.630 --> 01:35:44.610
And you will see grep being
used all over the place.

01:35:44.610 --> 01:35:52.700
It's super fast and great at
feeding the reasoning stage.

01:35:52.700 --> 01:35:54.340
And something else.

01:35:54.340 --> 01:35:58.980
So you talked about user context
as being something that you

01:35:58.980 --> 01:35:59.920
cannot control.

01:35:59.920 --> 01:36:03.340
So this is indeed something
you cannot control in the case

01:36:03.340 --> 01:36:04.780
of a regular LLM.

01:36:04.780 --> 01:36:06.640
But in the case
of a coding agent,

01:36:06.640 --> 01:36:08.380
you might have a set
of rules that you

01:36:08.380 --> 01:36:10.580
want your agent to abide by.

01:36:10.580 --> 01:36:12.400
So who has heard of AGENTS.md?

01:36:14.940 --> 01:36:16.260
Yeah, a lot of people.

01:36:16.260 --> 01:36:19.660
So this is a standardized
way to put everything

01:36:19.660 --> 01:36:23.140
that you would want your
agent to know at every session

01:36:23.140 --> 01:36:24.860
into a set of rules.

01:36:24.860 --> 01:36:28.700
And it's a way to
persist your preferences

01:36:28.700 --> 01:36:30.440
and have a control over them.

01:36:30.440 --> 01:36:34.980
So this is one characteristic
of coding agents.

01:36:34.980 --> 01:36:39.200
And something else that is
largely used in coding agents.

01:36:39.200 --> 01:36:44.940
So sometimes you have workflows
or tasks that you repeat.

01:36:44.940 --> 01:36:49.140
And you want a way
to execute them

01:36:49.140 --> 01:36:51.950
in a way that doesn't
require you to redescribe

01:36:51.950 --> 01:36:54.150
what you want all over again.

01:36:54.150 --> 01:36:56.650
So you have this
concept called skills,

01:36:56.650 --> 01:36:58.590
which you might
have heard as well,

01:36:58.590 --> 01:37:01.950
which is nothing else
than a place for you

01:37:01.950 --> 01:37:04.350
to write this down as a recipe.

01:37:04.350 --> 01:37:07.170
So you have some
prompting that goes there.

01:37:07.170 --> 01:37:13.090
Maybe the skill will need some
tool call or script to execute.

01:37:13.090 --> 01:37:15.830
So you have a folder
with a bunch of folders

01:37:15.830 --> 01:37:18.910
that are relevant to it.

01:37:18.910 --> 01:37:22.050
And I said this is something
that you want to write.

01:37:22.050 --> 01:37:23.110
Not really.

01:37:23.110 --> 01:37:27.410
Usually skills that are heavily
inferred from a given workflow,

01:37:27.410 --> 01:37:30.550
you would do something
end to end and then ask

01:37:30.550 --> 01:37:33.270
your coding agent to
reframe that as a skill.

01:37:33.270 --> 01:37:37.150
That would be an
efficient way to do it.

01:37:37.150 --> 01:37:40.470
And just giving some
names of the coding agent

01:37:40.470 --> 01:37:42.190
ecosystem out there.

01:37:42.190 --> 01:37:44.390
So you have a bunch of them.

01:37:44.390 --> 01:37:48.680
And one highlight in the
ecosystem that I would refer you

01:37:48.680 --> 01:37:53.120
to is Pi, which has the
benefit of being minimalistic

01:37:53.120 --> 01:37:55.200
in the harness that it provides.

01:37:55.200 --> 01:37:57.400
So typically, the
prompting that goes

01:37:57.400 --> 01:38:02.360
around each of these steps of
reason and acting and the rest

01:38:02.360 --> 01:38:04.840
are kept at a
minimum, so you are

01:38:04.840 --> 01:38:10.740
able to see your model
performing in its rawest form.

01:38:10.740 --> 01:38:13.320
And usually, we're going
to see it next week.

01:38:13.320 --> 01:38:16.840
It is also heavily used
to compare models based

01:38:16.840 --> 01:38:18.300
on a single coding harness.

01:38:21.160 --> 01:38:22.020
OK, great.

01:38:22.020 --> 01:38:24.400
There is one kind of tool
that I didn't mention

01:38:24.400 --> 01:38:26.020
and that is a growing use case.

01:38:26.020 --> 01:38:27.440
So computer use.

01:38:27.440 --> 01:38:31.280
You might want these agents
to control your computer.

01:38:31.280 --> 01:38:34.160
This is currently
super inefficient,

01:38:34.160 --> 01:38:36.880
because computer use
takes screenshots

01:38:36.880 --> 01:38:39.900
of what you see and tries
to predict the next action.

01:38:39.900 --> 01:38:43.580
So if you ever use computer
use as part of your agent,

01:38:43.580 --> 01:38:45.200
you might get frustrated.

01:38:45.200 --> 01:38:47.400
We're going to see
in two lectures

01:38:47.400 --> 01:38:50.880
that there might be some hope
around potential next steps

01:38:50.880 --> 01:38:53.280
of computer use.

01:38:53.280 --> 01:38:58.720
And I want to finish
by talking about what

01:38:58.720 --> 01:39:02.200
is bigger than a single
agent, multiple ones.

01:39:02.200 --> 01:39:05.480
So when you work
with coding agents,

01:39:05.480 --> 01:39:08.880
sometimes you see
subagents being spun up

01:39:08.880 --> 01:39:12.340
for tasks that do not
require a lot of reasoning

01:39:12.340 --> 01:39:14.240
but might be delegated.

01:39:14.240 --> 01:39:17.320
So it's one concept
that is quite present.

01:39:17.320 --> 01:39:18.600
It is a bit costly.

01:39:18.600 --> 01:39:21.480
Because every time you
spin up a subagent,

01:39:21.480 --> 01:39:24.500
you need to give it its
own context and so on.

01:39:24.500 --> 01:39:29.160
So it has a full loop of
agentic response being

01:39:29.160 --> 01:39:35.000
built to you, which is why
some minimalistic harnesses

01:39:35.000 --> 01:39:38.520
such as Pi boast
not supporting them

01:39:38.520 --> 01:39:40.520
or minimally supporting them.

01:39:40.520 --> 01:39:43.640
And there is another type that
is a growing use case that you

01:39:43.640 --> 01:39:46.730
might have heard,
agent swarms, where

01:39:46.730 --> 01:39:48.930
you have multiple
agents collaborating

01:39:48.930 --> 01:39:51.410
with each other
towards a given goal.

01:39:51.410 --> 01:39:55.850
So these agents, they have
a communication channel

01:39:55.850 --> 01:40:03.890
where they can share progress or
have cluster groups of efforts.

01:40:03.890 --> 01:40:06.050
And you might see
them in recent news

01:40:06.050 --> 01:40:09.090
sometimes for great
progress, for example,

01:40:09.090 --> 01:40:12.730
some mathematical advances
that were released recently.

01:40:12.730 --> 01:40:16.210
But also, that can
show vulnerabilities

01:40:16.210 --> 01:40:20.070
of giving a lot of power
to agents out there.

01:40:20.070 --> 01:40:23.610
So you might have heard
of the Hugging Face hack.

01:40:23.610 --> 01:40:29.210
And I would refer you to this
great reading of the model

01:40:29.210 --> 01:40:32.790
evaluation and threat research
group that is, I think,

01:40:32.790 --> 01:40:34.730
a great reading.

01:40:34.730 --> 01:40:37.210
And with that, I hope
you have a great weekend.

01:40:37.210 --> 01:40:40.560
[APPLAUSE]

